T1  - Muscle MRI Reveals Myotomal Denervation Patterns in Lumbar Radiculopathy
T3  - 33rd Annual Scientific Meeting of the European Society of Musculoskeletal Radiology, ESSR. Manchester United Kingdom.
A1  - Platzgummer H.
A1  - Vetchy V.
A1  - Rienmueller A.
Y1  - 2026//
N2  - Purpose or Learning Objective: To assess whether lower-extremity muscle magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) shows consistent myotomal denervation patterns in patients with suspected lumbar radiculopathy and whether these patterns add value when spine MRI shows multilevel degenerative changes. Methods or Background: In this retrospective single-center study, 47 consecutive patients who underwent lumbar spine MRI and bilateral lower-extremity muscle MRI were reviewed. Spine MRI was graded for nerve root contact/ displacement (grade 1/2), compression (grade 3), and for central canal and foraminal stenosis. Muscle MRI was assessed for edema on T2 Dixon water-only images as a marker of acute/subacute denervation. Muscle involvement was summarized by myotomal distribution, and associations between muscle edema and compression severity were tested using Fisher exact tests. Results or Findings: Nerve root compromise on spine MRI was present in 33 of 47 (70.2%) patients, including 21 of 47 (44.7%) with grade 3 compression and 12 of 47 (25.5%) with grade 1 and 2 contact/displacement. Muscle edema was frequent and increased with compression severity (90.5% in grade 3, 75.0% in grade 1/2, and 36.4% without compression). For grade 3 versus no compression, the odds ratio for any muscle edema was 14.7 (P = 0.003). Across cases, muscle MRI demonstrated recognizable myotomal denervation patterns in the lower-extremity muscles corresponding to the affected lumbar nerve root level. Conclusion(s): Adding muscle MRI to lumbar spine MRI enables recognition of myotomal denervation patterns in the leg muscles. It provides level-specific information beyond nerve root compression in spine MRI. These results support muscle MRI as a valuable complementary tool to improve level attribution and diagnostic confidence in routine assessment of lumbar radiculopathy, particularly when spine MRI is equivocal due to multilevel degenerative disease or when peripheral nerve involvement is part of the differential diagnosis.
