T1  - EARLY PERIPHERAL NERVE INVOLVEMENT IN ZELLWEGER SPECTRUM DISORDER: ELECTROPHYSIOLOGICAL AND ULTRASOUND FINDINGS
T3  - 26th National Congress of the Italian Association of Myology (AIM) and 16. Riunione Annuale Associazione Italiana per lo Studio del Sistema Nervoso Periferico, ASNP. Bologna Italy.
A1  - Bruno G.
A1  - Pascarella A.
A1  - Rubino A.
A1  - Nigro S.
A1  - Di Sarno I.
A1  - Ferrara D.
A1  - Esposito F.
A1  - Varone A.
A1  - De Martino B.
Y1  - 2026//
N2  - Zellweger Spectrum Disorder (ZSD) is a peroxisomal biogenesis disorder characterized by multisystem involvement, including central and peripheral nervous system abnormalities. Peripheral neuropathy is frequently reported, yet its structural and functional characterization in early infancy remains limited. We describe the electrophysiological and nerve ultrasound findings in a genetically confirmed neonatal ZSD case A neonate presenting with severe hypotonia, dysmorphic features, early-onset epilepsy, and neuroradiological abnormalities suggestive of a peroxisomal disorder underwent metabolic testing, next-generation sequencing (NGS), nerve conduction studies (NCS), and high-resolution nerve ultrasound Brain MRI showed white matter volume reduction, delayed myelination of pyramidal tracts, pachygyria, polymicrogyria, and germinolytic cysts. Plasma very long-chain fatty acids were markedly elevated (C26:0 4.97 muM/L; C26:0/C22:0 ratio 0.455). NGS identified compound heterozygous pathogenic variants in PEX6 (c.1947del; c.2245G > A), confirming ZSD. Nerve conduction studies demonstrated a sensorimotor polyneuropathy consistent with predominant dysmyelination. High-resolution nerve ultrasound revealed diffuse, symmetric enlargement of peripheral nerves with increased cross-sectional area and altered fascicular echotexture, indicating structural remodeling. This case highlights early and severe peripheral nerve involvement in ZSD. The combination of axonal dysfunction on electrophysiology and diffuse nerve enlargement on ultrasound suggests a complex neurodegenerative process possibly involving secondary dysmyelination. Multimodal assessment integrating nerve conduction studies and ultrasound may enhance phenotypic characterization and provide a non-invasive tool for monitoring peripheral nerve involvement in peroxisomal disorders.
