AN  - 2042178717
T1  - Novel Therapeutic Pathways in Multiple Sclerosis: Evidence for a Peripheral Nervous System-Led Model of Disease Initiation
A1  - Harris D.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Background: Multiple sclerosis (MS) is usually seen as an autoimmune disease of the central nervous system, but this study suggests it may begin in the peripheral nervous system. The author proposes that genetic susceptibility to abnormal DNA methylation- triggered by oral infections and mycotoxins-leads to autonomic imbalance, which in turn causes CNS damage. Notably, Helicobacter pylori bacteria can add methyl groups to myelin basic protein, potentially provoking an immune response that targets both foreign and self-antigens. Here, PNS initiation refers to detectable autonomic imbalance before any MRI or clinical signs of central demyelination appear. Objective(s): To evaluate whether autonomic/PNS factors plausibly precede CNS injury in an n-of-1 study. Method(s): An integrative n-of-1analysis combined genotyping microarray data (912,174 loci), curated non-major histocompatibility complex (non-MHC) MS risk variants, clinical outcomes (EDSS, MSQOL-54), and exposure history. Analyses included single-nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) overlap mapping, polygenic risk score (PRS)-like aggregation, and mechanistic visualization via a causal-loop framework. Limitation(s): Single-subject design, consumer genotyping platforms, and retrospective exposure assessment restrict generalizability. Additionally, H. pylori have been eradicated in Patient X, which limits the opportunity to confirm evidence of aberrantly methylated myelin basic protein. Future tests should be considered for active MS patients which include extracellular vesicles (EV)-based MBP proteomics with targeted parallel reaction monitoring (PRM) to quantify methylated MBP peptides, plus enzyme-linked immunosorbent spot (ELISpot) assay showing differential immune recognition of those same methyl marks. Result(s): Quantitative overlaps and PRS-like aggregates were computed from the Genome Wide Association Studies (GWAS) dataset; autonomic-loop modeling integrated genetic and clinical observations. Outcomes suggested convergence of methylation, immune, and environmental factors preceding central demyelination. Conclusion(s): Findings support a PNS-first cascade in MS pathogenesis in which sustained sympathetic activation and reduced vagal tone promote cytokine flux and blood-brain-barrier (BBB) disruption. The observations are consistent with a PNS-first cascade hypothesis but do not establish causation. Therefore, reframing MS with an autonomic focus may inform therapeutics such as vagal modulation, methylation support, and targeted eradication of triggering exposures. Further research should use standardized autonomic phenotyping to evaluate the reproducibility of the proposed pathways.Copyright © 2025, The Authors. All rights reserved.
