T1  - ULTRASONIC MANIFESTATIONS of CHARCOT-MARIE-TOOTH DISEASE: A CASE REPORT
T3  - 20th Congress of World Federation for Ultrasound in Medicine and Biology, WFUMB 2025. Kyoto Japan.
A1  - Wu Q.
A1  - Chen J.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Objectives: This case report is aimed at illustrating the value of ultrasonography (US) in a CMT1 (Charcot-marie-tooth) case with atypical symptoms. Method(s): The onset, clinical manifestations, ultrasonography and other auxiliary examinations, diagnosis, and treatment of a CMT case were analyzed and summarized. Result(s): A 30-year-old woman presented with symptoms of sore back for over 2 months and right ulnar numbness for 2 days, and ultrasonography was performed on the right ulnar nerve, which showed that the entire ulnar nerve was swollen and thickened, notably in the proximal heart segment. The thickest section was located in the middle of the upper arm, with a maximum cross-sectional area of 178 mm2. The nerve was continuous with increased epineurial echo, thickened nerve fascicles that retained the basic normal fascicular architecture, blurred echogenicity in part, and faint blood flow signals within the nerve trunk. The ultrasonographer suspected that the right ulnar nerve was not a solitary lesion and subsequently scanned the contralateral ulnar nerve, bilateral radial nerves, brachial plexuses, sciatic nerves, peroneal nerves, and tibial nerves. Except for the peroneal and sciatic nerves without obvious abnormalities, other peripheral nerves on both sides showed varying degrees of diffuse thickening-like changes. Genetic testing revealed that the patient had a heterozygous mutation in the 1, 4, 5-triphosphate inositol receptor type 3 (ITPR3) gene, and her mother had the same locus mutation. Combined with the patient's symptoms, signs, electromyography, neurosonography, MRI, genetic testing, nerve biopsy, and family history, the final diagnosis was CMT 1J. Conclusion(s): CMT is a kind of neuropathy with clinical and genetic heterogeneity. Neurosonology can provide high-resolution anatomic imaging of peripheral nerves, which can not only be used in the auxiliary diagnosis of CMT, but also provide an effective noninvasive assessment of the progression of peripheral neuropathy of CMT.
