T1  - Novel Tomographic Imaging of Peripheral Nerves with High-Resolution Ultrasonography Compared with High-Field MR (Tractography)
T3  - 29th Annual Scientific Meeting of the European Society of Musculoskeletal Radiology, ESSR. Rostock Germany.
A1  - Jengojan S.A.
A1  - Sorgo P.
A1  - Bodner G.
A1  - Kasprian G.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Purpose or Learning Objective: High-resolution ultrasonography (HRUS), and magnetic resonance neurography (MRN) are the imaging methods of choice for diagnosing peripheral nerves and peripheral neuropathies. With technical progress, tomographic ultrasonography (tUS) has made it possible to transform standard US systems into a tomographic imaging device comparable with multiplanar imaging techniques, such as computed tomography and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). The purpose of this prospective multimodal study was to compare three-dimensional (3D) tUS data with MRN (including magnetic resonance tractography [MRT]). Methods or Background: A total of 12 healthy volunteers and 12 patients with peripheral neuropathies underwent highresolution tUS and high-field MRN. A PIUR tUS Infinity System was used to produce 3D US volume. Peripheral nerves were reconstructed using an automated tracking algorithm. A high-field 3-T MRN was performed including diffusion tensor imaging and used to achieve nerve tract reconstruction (b-values of 0 and 700 s/mm ; 16 diffusion encoding directions). Results or Findings: The tUS results and the MRN (including MRT) provided comparable results. In cases of heavily altered nerves, tUS was able to depict the structurally altered nerve in 3D, whereas MRT failed to do so, due to changes in the nerve anisotropic characteristics. The 3D reconstruction of small and/or superficial nerves (diameter < 2 mm) was superior using tUS. On the contrary, the more deeply located nerves could be better reconstructed with MRN and MRT due to the limited depth visibility of HRUS. Conclusion(s): We found that tUS can reliably visualize normal peripheral nerves, as well as pathologies, and can therefore be considered a reliable technique for diagnostic assessment of peripheral nerve pathologies. Thus it could be used as a complement to MRN in the imaging of more superficial and/or small peripheral nerves and their pathologies, due to its highresolution US-based approach.
