Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Background/Purpose: Magnetic resonance neurography (MRN) is increasingly used due to its ability for non-invasively detecting and measuring peripheral nerve damage in various neuropathies. While MRN has been primarily applied in adults, there remains limited knowledge regarding its sensitivity and potential diagnostic benefits for children and adolescents. Thus, our objective was to investigate its utility in children and adolescents affected by metabolic neuropathies and to present preliminary findings from a small patient group with disorders related to mitochondrial long-chain fatty acid oxidation. Method(s): Prospective evaluation of patients with long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (LCHADD) and mitochondrial trifunctional protein deficiency (MTPD) via MRN, including T2-weighted and diffusion tensor imaging (DTI). So far, seven patients with LCHADD and five patients with MTPD between 6 and 17 years have been examined, of which eight presented with either clinical or electrophysiological signs of neuropathy, or both. T2 nerve lesion load and various DTI scalars were assessed, including fractional anisotropy (FA), which is an indicator for the structural integrity of peripheral nerves. Results were compared with eight healthy controls, carefully matched for age and BMI. Result(s): MRN revealed T2-hyperintense nerve lesions in patients with LCHADD and MTPD (ongoing analysis). Furthermore, mean sciatic nerve FA was significantly reduced in the patient group (0.47 +/- 0.07) compared with controls (0.62 +/- 0.05) (p < 0.01), indicating functional nerve changes. This was more apparent in patients with MTPD (LCHADD: 0.5 +/- 0.09; MTPD: 0.44 +/- 0.03) and mainly due to an increase in sciatic nerve radial diffusivity (RD) (p = 0.02). Conclusion(s): Our preliminary findings indicate that MRN is capable of detecting early neuropathic changes in patients with LCHADD and MTPD, even at a young age. Therefore, it has the potential to be a valuable supplementary tool for the detection and monitoring of peripheral neuropathy in this patient group.
