T1  - Prospective Validation of Peripheral Nerve-to-Muscle Ultrasound Ratios as Anatomical Biomarkers in Carpal Tunnel Syndrome
A1  - Garhwal D.
A1  - Patel P.D.
A1  - Ukabhai P.S.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Background: Carpal Tunnel Syndrome (CTS) is the most prevalent entrapment neuropathy of the upper limb, resulting from median nerve compression within the carpal tunnel. While nerve conduction studies (NCS) are considered the diagnostic gold standard, high-resolution ultrasonography (HRUS) has emerged as a valuable, non-invasive alternative for anatomical assessment. Conventional ultrasound parameters, such as median nerve cross-sectional area (CSA), have demonstrated good diagnostic accuracy; however, their interpretation can be influenced by patient-specific factors. Peripheral nerve-to-muscle ultrasound ratios may offer improved reliability and standardization across diverse populations. Aim(s): This study aimed to prospectively validate peripheral nerve-to-muscle ultrasound ratios as anatomical biomarkers for the diagnosis and grading of Carpal Tunnel Syndrome at a tertiary care hospital. Material(s) and Method(s): A prospective observational study was conducted on 40 clinically suspected CTS patients aged 20-70 years. All participants underwent detailed clinical examination, nerve conduction studies, and high-resolution ultrasonography using a 10-18 MHz linear probe. Median nerve CSA was measured at the carpal tunnel inlet and distal forearm, while thenar muscle CSA was assessed to calculate the Nerve-to-Muscle Ratio (NMR). Electrophysiological parameters were used to confirm diagnosis and grade severity. Data were analyzed using IBM SPSS version 25.0. Correlation coefficients, t-tests, and Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis were applied, with p< 0.05 considered significant. Result(s): The mean median nerve CSA was 13.52 +/- 2.45 mm2 on affected sides versus 8.10 +/- 1.88 mm2 on unaffected sides (p< 0.001). The mean NMR was significantly higher in affected limbs (1.88 +/- 0.36) than in unaffected ones (1.22 +/- 0.28, p< 0.001). NMR showed a strong correlation with CTS severity (r = 0.70, p< 0.001). ROC analysis revealed an optimal NMR cut-off of 1.45, with 90.62% sensitivity, 85.71% specificity, and AUC = 0.92 (95% CI: 0.84-0.99), indicating excellent diagnostic accuracy. Conclusion(s): Peripheral nerve-to-muscle ultrasound ratios, particularly NMR, are reliable, reproducible, and highly sensitive anatomical biomarkers for Carpal Tunnel Syndrome. They offer a rapid, non-invasive, and cost-effective alternative or adjunct to electrophysiological studies, enhancing diagnostic precision and early disease detection.Copyright ©2023Int. J. LifeSci.Biotechnol.Pharma.Res.
