T1  - Semi-automated MRI muscle volumetry and systematic muscle and nerve ultrasound in the diagnostic field of hereditary polyneuropathies: a longitudinal study
T3  - International Congress of Clinical Neurophysiology (ICCN). London United Kingdom.
A1  - Vockert K.L.
A1  - Lemainque T.
A1  - Kirschmann F.
A1  - Schulz J.B.
A1  - Winter N.
A1  - Dohrn M.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Background: Hereditary polyneuropathies are rare, genetically determined, and heterogeneous neurological disorders. They result in muscle atrophy, muscle weakness, and sensory deficits, primarily affecting the extremities. Precise identification and monitoring of disease progression are critical for diagnosis and evaluation of treatment effects, e.g. in clinical trials. Whole-body MRI enables comprehensive assessment of structural changes in muscles and surrounding tissues. Additionally, high-resolution ultrasound (HRUS) has proven to be a promising, non-invasive tool for characterizing peripheral nerve structures and superficial muscle groups. Objective(s): In this study, we examined 15 patients with hereditary neuropathies longitudinally over 4-5 years, focusing on changes in whole-body MRI and HRUS and their correlation with clinical and electrophysiological outcomes. Method(s): Fifteen patients (6 male, 9 female) underwent whole-body MRI (T1-weighted [T1w] turbo spin-echo sequences and mDixon Quant sequences for chemical shift-based determination of the proton density fat fraction [PDFF]) at two times between 2018 and 2023 (mean interval 4.73 +/- 0.33 years). High-resolution ultrasound (HRUS) was used to assess ultrasound pattern sum scores and muscle echogenicity. Clinical parameters included muscle strength (Medical Research Council scale), sudomotor function tests of hands and feet, nerve conduction studies of upper limb nerves, and the Charcot-Marie-Tooth Examination Score version 2 (CMTESv2). For volumetry, T1w-MRI datasets were manually segmented using ITK-Snap, measuring muscle volumes in the thigh and lower leg. Cylindrical regions of interest were placed in muscle, fat, and bone marrow tissues of the lower leg to determine mean T1w signal intensity and mean PDFF. Statistical analyses were conducted using GraphPad Prism 8. Result(s): Mean age at the second examination was 53.37 +/- 12.86 years (range 32-74 years). Genetic diagnoses included: CMT1A (n = 5; 33.33%), CMT1B (n = 2; 13.33%), CMT1C (n = 2; 13.33%), CMT1X (n = 3; 20%), CMT2 (n = 1; 6.67%), hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis (ATTRv, n = 1; 6.67%), and hereditary myoneuropathy (n = 1; 6.67%). During the observation period, the CMTESv2 of the entire cohort increased from 10.42 +/- 5.38 to 14.13 +/- 5.73 points on average (p = 0.0011). Muscle volumetry showed no significant changes for thigh (p = 0.9610) or lower leg muscles (p=0.6846). The current muscle volumes for the cohort averaged 3518.76 +/- 759.49 cm3 for the thigh and 1064.47 +/- 491.73 cm3 for the lower leg. There were no significant changes in muscle strength for foot dorsiflexors (p = 0.1211) or knee extensors (p = 0.2500). Analyses of mean T1w signal intensity, PDFF, and correlations with muscle echogenicity from HRUS are still pending. Updated results will be presented at the DGKN25. Conclusion(s): Over the observation period, thigh and lower leg muscle volumes showed no significant changes, reflecting the clinical picture of relatively stable muscle strength. Hereditary neuropathies are slowly progressive disorders. Our findings suggest that more sensitive longitudinal biomarkers are needed to capture disease progression and potential therapeutic responses. Future analyses will evaluate PDFF and compare muscle intensity with muscle echogenicity from ultrasound scans.Copyright © 2025
