T1  - A Case of Endophthalmitis Without Eye Pain
T3  - AACE Annual Meeting 2025. Orlando United States.
A1  - Anonymous.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Author Block: Carla Vasquez Espinosa, MD, University of New Mexico Description: Diabetes mellitus is a systemic condition that increases susceptibility to infections and can impair early detection. Endogenous endophthalmitis is an infection of the inner layers of the eye that presents with vision loss and eye pain, although up to 25% of cases may not experience eye pain. Case Report: A 64-year-old male with no significant medical history presented bilateral vision loss. He reported presence of floaters, blurry vision over 4 days. The patient denied headaches, eye pain, ocular trauma, or eye surgery. He did mention stubbing his left foot 1 week prior. Physical examination revealed bilateral conjunctival hyperemia and nonreactive pupils. Vision testing showed that the patient was just able to detect light. Patient had evidence of cellulitis on his left great toe. A brain and orbital MRI showed no significant findings. Laboratory studies revealed an HbA1c of 12%, and blood cultures positive for Methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus. Ophthalmology performed fundoscopy, which showed findings consistent with endophthalmitis. Discussion(s): Endophthalmitis is an infection of the inner layers of the eye, caused by either exogenous exposure or hematogenous dissemination (endogenous source). Endogenous endophthalmitis accounts for about 10% of all cases of endophthalmitis, with bacteremia rarely leading to endophthalmitis (<1%). Characteristic symptoms include vision loss or blurry vision, photophobia, and eye pain, although eye pain may be absent in up to 25% of cases. Risk factors for developing endogenous endophthalmitis include intravenous drug use, renal failure, immunocompromised status, and diabetes mellitus. Uncontrolled diabetes significantly impairs neutrophil bactericidal function and epidermal growth factor receptor signaling, compromising the immune response and healing. Conclusion(s): Studies indicate that ocular pain is equally frequent in non-diabetic and diabetic patients. However, poor glycemic control is known to affect peripheral nerves and pain perception. In this case, the patient developed endogenous endophthalmitis due to MSSA bacteremia, which originated from left foot cellulitis. His newly diagnosed, uncontrolled diabetes increased his risk for this severe infection and likely hindered early detection due to the absence of ocular pain.Copyright © 2025
