Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Introduction: Peripheral nerve tumors refer to a wide spectrum of diagnoses that can be encountered in clinical practice. These tumors can be broadly divided into neurogenic and non-neurogenic entities, with each group further divided into benign and malignant lesions. Neurogenic PNTs can range from benign sporadic neurofibromas and schwannomas to malignant PNTs. Non-neurogenic PNTs are less common and include neuroma, peripheral nerve ganglion, intraneural venous malformation, lipomatosis of nerve, or nerve focal hypertrophy. Method(s): We present a pictorial review of the various lesions of the peripheral nerve, as well as rare tumors with challenges in the interpretation of imaging findings. CT may be useful for the more precise evaluation of calcifications and osteolytic lesions. MRI is the imaging modality of choice, as its multiple planes and contrasts allow for the precise anatomic localization of a mass. Various techniques such asMicroneurography, Contrast-enhanced ultrasound, Spinal ganglion morphometry, MRI spectroscopy, diffusion, diffusion tensor imaging and tractography play a role in the diagnosis of pathology of PNS. But the question is: Are these techniques applicable in daily work? Results: Most common pathology that affects the peripheral nerves is due to compression, trauma or tumor. Clinical examination and electrophysiologic studies are the traditional mainstay of diagnostic work-up; Ct is useful in the etiological diagnosis of some entrapment syndromes, particularly if a bone-based, tumoral or vascular cause is suspected: its good spatial resolution, excellent bone contrast and the ability to perform CT angiography are advantages in this situation. CT can also be used to diagnose peripheral nerve tumors: can easily detect a mass, although it may be difficult to confirm its neural origin. Ultrasonography and magnetic resonance imaging provide spatial information regarding the affected nerve and its surroundings, often assisting in narrowing the differential diagnosis and guiding treatment. Conclusion(s): The techniques used to examine the peripheral nervous system, either using ultrasound or MRI, have the clear advantage of being able to examine the perineural environment, which is not easily assessed by clinical examination alone or by electroneuromyography. We illustrate the spectrum of peripheral nerve tumors and their mimics, emphasizing key distinguishing features to provide optimal MRN interpretation that enhances diagnostic thinking and therapeutic management.
