N2  - Introduction: Peripheral nerve injuries pose significant clinical challenges due to their complexity, regenerative limitations, and functional impact on patients. Recently artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a transformative tool in peripheral nerve reconstruction, enhancing diagnosis, planning, intraoperative precision, and outcome prediction. Material(s) and Method(s): The authors describe their experience in the preclinical and clinical use of AI tools in peripheral nerve reconstruction. Result(s): AI tools can be used to personalize devices, potentially useful for peripheral nerve repair. AI-based image analysis demonstrated high accuracy in identifying nerve structures using ultrasound and MRI, with deep learning models such as convolutional neural networks (CNNs) automating nerve segmentation and morphological assessment. Machine learning algorithms predicted regeneration outcomes based on intraoperative and electrophysiological data, aiding surgical decision-making. Robotic-assisted microsurgery integrated with AI-based navigation improved operative precision, while virtual reality (VR) and AI-driven simulation platforms enhanced surgical training in nerve repair. Discussion(s): AI applications in peripheral nerve reconstruction show substantial potential to augment diagnostic accuracy, optimize surgical planning, and improve educational platforms. Automated nerve segmentation streamlines preoperative mapping and supports personalized treatment approaches. Integrating AI with robotic microsurgery and VR-based training can further enhance surgical precision and skill acquisition. However, realworld implementation requires addressing challenges related to data privacy, model generalizability, regulatory approvals, and interdisciplinary collaboration for robust clinical translation. Conclusion(s): Artificial intelligence is poised to revolutionize peripheral nerve reconstruction by offering new therapeutic strategies and by improving imaging analysis, surgical planning, and training.
