T1  - ATYPICAL BELL'S PALSY WITH COEXISTING HEMIPLEGIC MIGRAINE: A DIAGNOSTIC CHALLENGE
T3  - Annual Meeting of the Society of General Internal Medicine, SGIM 2025. Hollywood, FL United States.
A1  - Khan Z.
A1  - Sarder L.
A1  - Trube J.
A1  - Hernandez K.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - CASE: A 40-year-old female with a history of migraines presented with right-sided facial numbness and droop for one day, as well as right upper and right lower extremity weakness, accompanied by a headache that was ongoing for two days. On presentation, her neurologic examination was significant for loss of the right nasolabial fold, and right facial droop with sparing of the forehead muscles. Motor strength was 4 out of 5 in the right upper and right lower extremity. Non-contrast computed tomography (CT) of the head, CT angiogram of the head and neck, CT cerebral perfusion and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain were negative for acute pathology. Due to her persistent headache, she was treated with NSAIDs, resulting in improvement of her right sided weakness. Her facial droop persisted and she was diagnosed with atypical Bell's palsy, with her facial symptoms resolving after initiation of oral steroids. She was discharged on hospital day 3 with no neurologic deficits. IMPACT/DISCUSSION: Bell's palsy is a peripheral nerve disorder that typically causes complete, unilateral facial paralysis. In rare cases, it can present atypically due to a partial lesion at the distal facial nerve, located between the deep and superficial lobes of the parotid gland near the pes anserinus. This spares the temporal branch to the frontalis muscle, resulting in lower facial paralysis with preserved forehead movement. When atypical, Bell's palsy can create a complex clinical presentation, particularly when combined with hemiplegic migraines; a subtype of migraines characterized by reversible hemiparesis. Managing both conditions independently suggests the presence of concomitant pathologies. Although this overlap is uncommon, its occurrence presents the diagnostic challenge of recognizing coexisting disorders. CONCLUSION(S): The coexistence of atypical Bell's palsy and hemiplegic migraines presents a rare but diagnostically challenging scenario requiring careful evaluation. Recognizing these overlapping conditions stresses the importance of considering multiple pathologies in complex presentations, allowing us to guide accurate diagnosis and management.
