T1  - EXPLORING the IMMUNOGENETIC LINK between YOUNG-ONSET HODGKIN LYMPHOMA and MULTIPLE SCLEROSIS: A TWO-CASE REPORT on DISEASE CO-OCCURRENCE
T3  - 30th Congress of theEuropean Hematology Association Annual Congress, EHA2025. Milan Italy.
A1  - PIrvu E.
A1  - Ilie N.
A1  - Marian I.
A1  - Grasu D.
A1  - Malenda A.
A1  - B canu M.
A1  - Andreescu M.
A1  - Popov V.M.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Background Hodgkin lymphoma (HL) and Multiple Sclerosis (MS) share immunogenetic susceptibilities, with studies linking young-onset HL to increased MS risk with the inverse also being observed. HL involves malignant B-cell transformation in an immunosuppressive microenvironment, while MS is driven by autoreactive T- and B-cell-mediated neuroinflammation. Shared risk factors include genetic predisposition, immune dysfunction, and Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). The HLADRB1* 15:01 allele, a major MS risk factor, is also linked to HL, reinforcing common susceptibility. A chromosome 3p24.1 variation, associated with the EOMES gene, further connects both diseases through its role in cell-mediated immunity. Immunohistochemical studies reveal similar immune activation patterns, suggesting common immunopathological mechanisms. Aims To explore the temporal relationship between young-onset HL and MS, focusing on chemotherapy-induced immune dysregulation as a potential autoimmune trigger. The study examines clinical and biological correlations, emphasizing immunohistochemical findings and inflammatory marker expression. Methods A retrospective case series from 2021-2024 at Colentina Clinical Hospital included two male patients aged 30 and 32 years old with stage 4B Ann-Arbour HL-one with pulmonary, the other with hepatic involvement. Both developed neurological symptoms during chemotherapy, leading to MS diagnoses. One had positive Viral Capsid Antigen (VCA) IgG, confirming prior Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. Immunohistochemistry confirmed CD15, CD30, and PAX5 positivity in both cases, with one also positive for CD20. Both received six cycles of Brentuximab vedotin + AVD chemotherapy, followed by radiotherapy. Neurological symptoms emerged after 2-3 cycles of chemotherapy: one developed persistent muscle contractions; the other experienced bilateral paresthesia, diplopia, vertigo, and blurred vision. MRI revealed demyelinating lesions, lumbar puncture showed oligoclonal bands, and EMG confirmed peripheral nerve damage. Additional evaluations detected optic nerve myelinopathy in one pacient and vestibular deficits in both. Differential diagnoses ruled out alternative autoimmune, infectious, and metabolic causes. Results A clear temporal link emerged between HL treatment and MS onset, suggesting chemotherapy-induced immune dysregulation as a trigger. Immunohistochemistry showed significant B-cell marker expression (PAX5, BCL6, CD20) in HL tissue, indicating aberrant B-cell activation. Persistent T-cell activation (CD30) mirrored CD4+/CD8+ T-cell activity in MS. Both diseases exhibited chronic inflammation, with markers like CD68 and CD15 suggesting a pro-inflammatory microenvironment. Chemotherapy-induced immune alterations may facilitate MS onset, potentially exacerbated by EBV reactivation and blood-brain barrier disruption, making it more permeable to immune cells. Summary/Conclusion These cases highlight the interplay between HL and MS onset. Immune dysregulation in HL, compounded by treatmentinduced immunosuppression, may unmask or accelerate autoimmune conditions. While definitive causality remains unproven, the findings suggest a strong link requiring further genetic and immunological research. Understanding these mechanisms may improve therapeutic strategies, reducing autoimmune complications in HL patients.
