Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Background and aims: Diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN) is a common complication of diabetes mellitus (DM). There is evidence that the central nervous system (CNS) is involved in DPN. This study aims to evaluate the spinal cord cross-sectional area (SCA) in DPN and its relationship with neuropathic pain and vibration perception threshold (VPT). Method(s): This cross-sectional study (SpINDLE: ISRCTN11328492) analyzed the spinal cord MRI of 72 participants across five groups: diabetes with and without DPN, peripheral neuropathies without DM, multiple sclerosis, and healthy controls. The cervical and thoracic SCA were measured using NeuROI and normalized for height. The normalized SCA was compared between the groups using one-way ANOVA and the correlation between SCA, pain scores, and VPT was assessed using Spearman Correlation test. Result(s): The mean cervical and thoracic SCA were not significantly different between the groups. Participants with DPN (n = 14) showed a trend toward reduced SCA compared to healthy controls (n = 18) (cervical: 76.15 vs. 79.80 mm2 and thoracic: 46.62 vs. 51.24 mm2) but not compared to the other groups. In the DPN group, reduced thoracic SCA, but not cervical SCA, significantly correlated with higher neuropathic pain scores (p = 0.027). No significant correlation was found between SCA and VPT. Conclusion(s): DPN patients with higher levels of neuropathic pain seem to have a smaller thoracic cord. Therefore, structural changes in the spinal cord may have a role in DM-related neuropathic pain. Further research is needed to understand the association between spinal cord changes in DM and severity of DPN.Copyright © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
