N2  - Background and Aim: Ataxia-telangiectasia (AT) is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by progressive cerebellar degeneration, oculocutaneous telangiectasia, immunodeficiency and susceptibility to cancer as well as radiation toxicity. It is caused by pathogenic variants in the ataxia-telangiectasia-mutated (ATM) gene. MRI brain in patients typically demonstrates cerebellar volume loss, compensatory enlargement of the 4th ventricle, scattered small white matter T2 hypointensities are often identified in patients with AT indicating tiny hemosiderin deposits related to thrombosis or vascular leaks from telangiectatic vessels. In patients with AT on SWI imaging, the dentate nuclei can have deficient iron signals, which otherwise should be visible in SWI. Case Report: We report a rare case of a 15-year-old female with ataxia telangiectasia with iron deficient signals in the bilateral dentate nuclei in the MRI brain sequence-SWI. A 15-year-old female with a normal birth and developmental history till the age of 2 years after which the patient presented with a progressive disease in the form of cerebellar ataxia, ataxic dysarthria, peripheral neuropathy, recurrent falls, oculomotor apraxia, recurrent respiratory tract infections, myoclonus, chorea, conjunctival telangiectasias. Laboratory tests showed mild iron deficiency anemia and serum alfa fetoprotein levels- 195 ng/ ml, NCS of all 4 limbs showed bilateral axonal sensorimotor polyneuropathy, MRI brain with SWI was done, and it showed marked cerebellar atrophy with cerebral microbleeds and loss of iron signals in the bilateral dentate nucleus. Discussion and Conclusion(s): Recognition of iron-depleted signals in the bilateral dentate nuclei may help in further radiological confirmation of this disease.
