A1  - Bendszus M.
A1  - Kopf S.
A1  - Kurz F.T.
A1  - Jende J.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background and aims: Skin auto-fluorescence (sAF), as a marker for intradermal deposition of advanced glycation-endproducts (AGEs), predicts microangiopathic complications in type 2 diabetes (T2D) such as distal sensorimotor diabetic polyneuropathy (DSPN). This study aimed to investigate whether changes of the structural integrity of the sciatic nerve are related to skin advanced glycation end products, the latter being more tightly correlated to vascular complications compared to circulating AGEs. Material(s) and Method(s): 62 individuals with T2D (20 women, 42 men), 29 with DSPN (7 women, 22 men), 33 without DSPN (nDSPN) and 10 healthy (HC) participants of similar age, BMI and gender distribution underwent clinical, neurological, and serological assessments as well as diffusion tensor imaging of the right sciatic nerve to determine the fractional anisotropy (FA) of the sciatic nerve as a measure of the nerve's structural integrity. Arterial stiffness was measured by pulse wave velocity (PWV). Result(s): sAF (HC 2.1+/-0.25 [arbitrary unit], nDSPN 2.3+/-0.47, DSPN 2.6+/-0.43; p=0.005) was higher in individuals with DSPN compared to HC (p=0.010) and compared to nDSPN (p=0.035). Partial correlation analysis of the sciatic nerve's FA controlled for eGFR and age revealed a negative correlation with sAF in all individuals with T2D (r=-0.30, p=0.039). In DSPN, sAF correlated positively with hsTNT (r=0.58, p=0.005) and with PWV (r=0.52, p=0.007). In DSPN, the sciatic nerve's FA correlated negatively with PWV (r=-0.47,p=0.010). Conclusion(s): This study is the first to demonstrate that intradermal AGE deposition and arterial stiffness are tightly correlated with the structural integrity of proximal peripheral nerves in individuals with T2D.
