T1  - Mitigating Peripheral Nerve Stimulation in Cardiac Diffusion Tensor Imaging (cDTI) with Time-optimal Diffusion Encoding Gradient Waveform Design
T3  - CMR 2024 Global CMR Conference. QEII Centre, London United Kingdom.
A1  - Hannum A.
A1  - Loecher M.
A1  - Setsompop K.
A1  - Ennis D.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background: Cardiac diffusion tensor imaging (cDTI) is a tool for monitoring microstructural remodeling in the heart.1 cDTI encodes diffusion sensitivity via diffusion encoding gradient waveforms that simultaneously null sensitivity to velocity (M1) and acceleration (M2). Diffusion weighting combined with the longer TEs necessitated by M1+M2 compensation contributes to cDTI being signal starved. TEs may also be prolonged owing to the need to derate gradient slewing during the application of the large diffusion encoding gradients to avoid peripheral nerve stimulation (PNS). In fact, recently available ultra-high performance gradients (200 T/m & 200 T/m/s) are an excellent choice for cDTI, but PNS has to be managed. Balancing the competing needs of M1+M2 nulling, avoiding PNS, and minimizing TE cannot be resolved by conventional gradient waveform design approaches. Previous work has demonstrated the use of numerical gradient optimization (GrOpt2) for time-optimal arbitrarily-shaped gradient waveform design2,3 subject to constraints. The objective of this work was to evaluate the minimum TE achievable when using time-optimal gradient waveform designs that mitigates PNS for cDTI. Method(s): Simulations for both a 45/200-System and a 200/200-System (GMax/SRMax, Table 1A) evaluated traditional trapezoidal (TRAP) and GrOpt waveforms.3 We simulated four conditions: TRAP-45/43, TRAP-200/43, GrOpt-45/200, and GrOpt-200/200. The SRmax of the TRAP waveform required de-rating to limit PNS and 43T/m/s is typically used. The PNS model was based on the International Engineering Standard (IEC 60601-2-33)4 with the following coefficients: alpha=0.333m, r=23.4T/s, Smin=60T/ms, c=334mus. Diffusion encoding gradient waveforms were designed to be within 1% of the target b-values = 250, 500, 750, 1000 s/mm2 and with PNS threshold of 0.95. An M1+M2 compensated spin-echo EPI sequence was simulated for each condition at 1.5, 2, and 2.5 mm2 resolutions (Table 1B). TE and percent TE (%TE) reported. Result(s): GrOpt enables using GMax and SRMax for longer intervals, thereby shortening the TE while also staying under the PNS threshold (Fig. 1). For the range of b-values and resolutions investigated (Fig. 2), GrOpt consistently outperforms the TRAP waveforms by reducing TEs between 14-58ms (12-42%) and avoiding PNS. For a b=500 and 2mm resolution cDTI sequence (Fig. 1), GrOpt demonstrates larger TE reductions in a 200/200-System [31%] vs. 45/200-System [19%]. GrOpt TEs in a 45/200-System are close to TRAP TEs in a de-rated 200/200-System. Conclusion(s): GrOpt minimizes TE and avoids PNS directly for cDTI. Future work will enable rapid on-the-scanner adjustment of PNS with GrOpt in vivo. TE reductions with GrOpt open opportunities to increase image resolution or increase sensitivity to diffusion by increasing the b-value without the TE penalties. Overall, this study demonstrates that GrOpt has the potential to fully utilize hardware capabilities for cDTI encoding, thereby maximizing the SNR and encoding efficiency. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Table 1. System and Image Acquisition Parameters for Simulation Experiments. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Author Disclosure: A Hannum: Nothing to disclose; M Loecher: N/A; K Setsompop: N/A; D Ennis: N/ACopyright © 2024
