T1  - Ataxia telangiectasia genome instability syndrome: A case report
T3  - 8th Annual Conference of the Movement Disorders Society of India. Ahmedabad India.
A1  - Kaur S.
A1  - Pandian J.D.
A1  - Jaison V.
A1  - Sahonta R.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background: Ataxia telangiectasia (AT) is a rare autosomal recessive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by cerebellar degeneration, telangiectasia, immunodeficiency, cancer susceptibility, and radiation sensitivity. Death occurs due to cancer and infection. Counseling plays an important role as there is no cure for the disease. Case Report: We report a case of a 14-year-old girl, born out of nonconsanguineous marriage, presenting with complaints of inability to maintain balance, frequent falls, abnormal involuntary dystonic movement involving the head, neck, trunk, and all four limbs, and slurring of speech for last 4 years. On examination, she had ocular telangiectasia and truncal ataxia [Figure 1]. Magnetic resonance imaging showed diffuse cerebellar atrophy and atrophy of the superior cerebellar peduncle. Nerve conduction study was suggestive of axonal neuropathy. Laboratory investigations revealed raised AFP levels. Whole exome sequencing detected compound heterozygous variants [c.1802 + 1del; c.6310T>C ((p. Trp2104Arg)] in the ATM gene. Patient was given symptomatic and supportive treatment (clonazepam, vitamin E, balance exercises, and speech therapy). Discussion(s): AT is mainly a clinical diagnosis. It should be suspected in the presence of ataxia of early onset in infants, oculocutaneous telangiectasia, and abnormal cellular or humoral immunity in early childhood. Some may also have uncommon signs and symptoms, such as dystonic head movements as seen in our case. The absence of awareness of AT and the late onset of telangiectasia hamper the early diagnosis of the condition. No definite treatment is available. Management includes a multidisciplinary approach, but the prognosis is poor and depends primarily on the respiratory infections and susceptibility to cancer.
