T1  - An Anatomic Study of the Subarachnoid Space Surrounding the Trigeminal Ganglion in Horses in Preparation for a Computer-Navigated Glycerol Rhizotomy in Equids with Trigeminal-Mediated Headshaking
T3  - 2024 ACVS Surgery Summit. Phoenix, AZ United States.
A1  - Becker R.
A1  - Haenssgen K.
A1  - Precht C.
A1  - Khoma O.
A1  - Hlushchuk R.
A1  - Koch C.
A1  - Kaessmeyer S.
A1  - De Preux M.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Equine trigeminal-mediated headshaking is a painful neuropathic disorder comparable to trigeminal neuralgia in humans. The human trigeminal ganglion is enclosed by a dural recess called the trigeminal cave, in which the ganglion is surrounded by a cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)-filled subarachnoid space. During glycerol rhizotomy, glycerol is percutaneously injected in this CSF-filled space to cause selective destruction of pain fibers. The aim of this study was to explore if a CSF-filled subarach-noid space around the trigeminal ganglion exists in horses. Six equine cadaver heads were investigated for CSF accumulation around the ganglion with a 3 Tesla MRI. Subsequently, a polymer-based radiopaque contrast agent was injected through the porus trigeminus into the subarachnoid space. The exact delineation and the vol-ume of the contrast agent accumulation were determinedon subsequent micro-computed tomographic scans and segmentation. Finally, the distribution of the contrast agent within the subarachnoid space was examined histo-logically in three specimens. All 12 trigeminal ganglia included in this study were surrounded by a subarach-noid space forming a trigeminal cistern with a mean vol-ume of 0.31 mL (+/-SD: 0.11 mL). Notably, contrast agent distribution along peripheral nerves, (i.e., ophthalmic, maxillary, and/or mandibular nerve), was evident in 7 of 12 specimens. Limitations include the cadaveric nature and the small sample size of the study. Overall, a sub-arachnoid space surrounding the trigeminal ganglion exists in the horse and could be targeted for glycerol rhi-zotomy to address trigeminal-mediated headshaking. However, the clinical relevance of contrast agent distribu-tion along the peripheral nerves remains to be assessed.
