A1  - Jende J.
A1  - Kender Z.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background and aims: Distal symmetric polyneuropathy (DSPN) and nephropathy were previously classified as microangiopathic diabetes complications. However, today, the concept of a common pathogenesis with a central role of vascular function is being questioned. Our aim was to explore the relationship between the structural integrity of the sciatic nerve and renal function in individuals with Type 1 (T1D) and Type 2 diabetes (T2D). Material(s) and Method(s): A total of 56 individuals with T2D (18 females, 38 males) and 14 individuals with T1D (8 females, 6 males) were subjected to clinical parameters for glucose control, kidney function and neuropathy, as well as MR-Neurography (MRN) of the sciatic nerve. MRN was performed with diffusion tensor imaging, and fractional anisotropy (FA) was measured as a marker for structural integrity. Result(s): In individuals with T2D and DSPN, both the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) (DSPN: 82.2 +/- 14.9 ml/min/1.73 m2; non-DSPN: 93.1 +/- 12.2; p=0.011) and the FA of the sciatic nerve (DSPN: 0.40 +/- 0.05; non-DSPN: 0.45 +/- 0.05; p=0.001) were lower compared to those without DSPN. However, no difference was observed in the albumincreatinine ratio between individuals with and without DSPN in the T2D group. Among individuals with T1D, a positive correlation was found between the FA of the sciatic nerve and GFR (r=0.55, p=0.042). Additionally, when controlling for age, a partial correlation analysis revealed a positive correlation between FA of the sciatic nerve and GFR among individuals with T2D (r=0.29, p=0.034), as well as across the combined groups of individuals with T1D and T2D (r=0.33, p=0.005). Conclusion(s): In individuals with T1D or T2D, renal function relates to structural integrity of the sciatic nerve, serving as an indicator of peripheral nerve damage. Since individuals with severe kidney insufficiency were excluded from the study, the results imply that common pathogenic mechanisms affecting peripheral nerves and glomeruli, can be identified in individuals with diabetes even prior to the development of diabetic nephropathy.
