N2  - Introduction: Chronic autoimmune-mediated neuropathies have been rarely reported after SARS-CoV-2-vaccination. We present the first high-resolution nerve ultrasound (HRUS) and MR-Neurography (MRN) report on a chronic immune-mediated neuropathy after SARS-CoV-2-infection. Material(s) and Method(s): We report a case of a 33-year-old male with a manifestation of chronic immune-mediated neuropathy resembling a chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) after SARS-CoV2. We describe findings of nerve conductions studies (NCS), HRUS, MRN and corneal confocal microscopy (CCM). Result(s): One day after the initial symptoms of the SARS-CoV-2-infection, the patient reported numbness beginning in his feet extending to his genital region. Symptoms deteriorated to a bilateral distal paraparesis with overall disability sum score (ODSS) of 5. Nerve conduction studies showed prolonged F-wave latencies of tibial nerves. Cerebrospinal fluid showed a cytoalbuminar dissociation with increased protein level (81mg/dl after 2 months and 78,1 mg/dl after 3 months) and normal cell count. Patient was treated with cumulative 145 g intravenous immunoglobulins (IVIg) over five days. After an initial improvement (INCAT/ODSS 3) a disease deterioration was detected (ODSS 4). Then, a chronic immune-mediated neuropathy was diagnosed and treatment with 70g IVIg every 4 weeks was initiated. Follow up examinations nine months after first manifestation showed clinical improvement (ODSS 3). NCS showed further prolonged F-wave latencies (mean: 64,8ms tibial nerves; 59,3ms fibular nerves). HRUS showed a normal cross-sectional area of all peripheral nerves. CCM revealed normal axonal markers and a cell count of 126 cells/mm2. MRN showed hypertrophy and increased T2-weighted signal intensity of the lumbosacral plexus correlating with the NCS findings. Nerve lesions extended beyond the plexus along the left sciatic nerve, displaying an increase in T2-weighted signal and mild enlargement primarily affecting the tibial portion at mid- to distal thigh level Discussion: We present the first characterization of a chronic-immune-mediated neuropathy after SARS-CoV-2-infection using HRUS and MRN. The underlying inflammation seems to be located in the proximal roots correlating with NCS whereas HRUS did not show any peripheral nerve enlargement. The patient responded to IVIg therapy. Our study highlights the need for thorough NCS and implementation of MRN in cases of immune-mediated neuropathy after SARS-CoV2.Copyright © 2023
