N2  - Introduction: MR Neurography (MRN) has increasingly been used in clinical practice in the diagnostic evaluation of abnormalities of the peripheral nerves and plexuses. Method(s): Five-hundred-thirty-four (534) MRN (232 brachial plexus, 207 lumbosacral plexus and 95 peripheral nerves) were performed in a 10 year period. Study protocol included 2D T1W, T2W Fat-Saturated (FS) and 3D-isotropic STIR sequences. In most cases it was supplemented with T1W-FS sequences after contrast medium administration. Difusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) fndings are not discussed in the current report. Findings suggestive of nerve abnormalities were nerve enlargement, deviation, or discontinuity, disruption of the fascicular pattern, T2 hyperintensities, contrast enhancement (blood-nerve barrier breakdown), and muscle denervation (acute or chronic). Result(s): Detailed anatomy of the peripheral nerves and plexuses was depicted in 513/534 exams (225/232 brachial plexus, 199/207 lumbosacral plexus and 89/95 peripheral nerves). Evaluation of smaller nerves was also feasible, including long thoracic nerve, grate occipital nerve, dorsal scapular nerve, phrenic nerve and the nerves of the extremities. Nerve/plexus compression/deviation was seen in 81 patients due to bone abnormalities, fractures, muscle entrapment, efusions, aneurysms, tumors and synovitis. Plexus injury was diagnosed in 38 patients (history of trauma or surgery), and neuroma formation was found in 4. Wallerian degeneration was noticed in 19 studies. Neoplasms of the peripheral nerves and plexuses were found in 31 patients, including schwannomas, neurofbromas, malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors, perineuromas (3 patients) infltration from lymphoma or leukemia (8), Ewing sarcoma of the brachial (1) and sacral plexus (1), and radiation induced malignant transformation of peripheral nerve stealth tumor (1). Acute or chronic Inflammation was seen in 69 patients (multiple plexus involvement in 9), including viral (ex. herpes) neuropathy, Parsonage Turner Syndrome, Chronic Infammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy, diabetic polyneuropathy, Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease. Regression in follow up was found in 19 patients with acute inflammation. Postradiation neuropathy was evident in 5 patients. Discussion(s): MRN is a reproducible and objective technique in the diagnostic armamentarium of peripheral nerves and plexuses abnormalities, supplementing clinical evaluation and electrodiagnostic testing. It is a valuable tool for the localization, evaluation and diagnosis of peripheral nerves and plexuses abnormalities.
