Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Case Report: Inferior vena cava syndrome (IVCS) is a consequence of the blockage or external compression of the inferior vena cava (IVC), and it is difficult to diagnose. Case Presentation: 78-year-old male with a background of obesity, Type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and coronary artery disease presented with a 2-month history of progressive bilateral leg swelling, scrotal swelling, and back pain. He denied orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea but was unable to lie flat because of his back pain. Vital signs were stable. Physical examination revealed anasarca, bilateral 3+ pitting lower extremity edema extending up to the umbilicus, and peripheral neuropathy. Blood work showed calcium of 12.1 mg/dL, creatinine of 2.5 mg/dL with an eGFR of 24, lactic acidosis 8.5 mmol/L, CRP 42.50 mg/L with normal WBC count on admission. Alpha fetoprotein, PSA, s-hcg, CA 19-9, CEA were negative. LDH, s2-microglobulin was elevated pointing towards a possible malignancy. Transthoracic echocardiogram was within normal limits. CT scan of the abdomen showed a large right retroperitoneal soft tissue mass with retroperitoneal and bilateral iliac chain adenopathy, and moderate right hydroureteronephrosis with the right mid ureter obstructed due to the right retroperitoneal mass. We were unable to administer contrast because of the renal function, and MRI was difficult to perform because the patient was unable to lie supine due to his back pain. The IVC was not visible distally with duplex ultrasonography due to a shadowing artefact. Testicular ultrasound was normal. The oncology service was consulted, and they recommended taking a biopsy for diagnosis. Interventional radiology placed a right nephrostomy drain and biopsied a retroperitoneal lymph node. Unfortunately, the biopsy result was non-conclusive. The patient later developed unstable symptomatic bradycardia, refused further treatment, and opted for comfort care. Hence, we were unable to determine the nature of the retroperitoneal mass. [Figure presented] Discussion: IVCS rarely presents as a primary diagnosis, hence, has a wide differential diagnosis. There are no pathognomonic tests to diagnose IVCS, however, the obstruction of the IVC can lead to a decrease in the venous return with a subsequent reduced perfusion of end organs. Early detection of the IVCS can lead to early malignancy detection and a better prognosis.Copyright © 2023 Southern Society for Clinical Investigation.
