N2  - Background Multisystem inflammatory syndrome associated with SARS-CoV2 is well described in the pediatric population, but rarely seen in adults. Case 50-year-old female with no significant history presented with fatigue, acute chest pain following one week of fevers. SARS-CoV2 testing was positive and CT angiogram was negative for aortic dissection. She rapidly deteriorated into cardiogenic shock (lactate 5.9), with initial cardiac index 1.45 and low filling pressures. Despite IV fluids and escalating vasopressors, cardiogenic shock worsened. Additionally, troponin was 0.28, CRP 26, and CK persistently elevated in the 4,000 U/L range. Physical exam was significant for peripheral neuropathy. TTE showed global hypokinesis, LVEF 35% with moderate pericardial effusion. Coronary angiogram showed no obstructive disease. Toxicology, C Burnetii, Parvovirus B19, Coxsackie, CMV, parasite smear, blood cultures were unrevealing. IABP was placed via right femoral artery, complicated by rapid atrial fibrillation, respiratory failure, and worsening shock requiring VA ECMO. Peripheral access was attempted, but due to inadequate perfusion, central VA ECMO placed. Endomyocardial biopsy was obtained showing no evidence of myocarditis. COVID-19 infection was treated with dexamethasone, remdesivir and tocilizumab. VA ECMO was decannulated post-op day 5 with LVEF 59%. Cardiac MRI showed no evidence of late gadolinium enhancement of LV or RV. Decision-making Mechanical circulatory support (MCS) was a bridge to recovery during work up for cardiogenic shock. Patient met CDC criteria for multisystem inflammatory syndrome following SARS-CoV2 in adults (MIS-A): fever, SARS-CoV2 infection, severe cardiac illness with shock, new-onset neurologic symptoms and inflammation. Absence of severe respiratory failure helps distinguish MIS-A from SARSCoV2 alone. Treatments in case reports include IVIG, corticosteroids and tocilizumab. Rarely, MCS is required for support of cardiogenic shock in MIS-A. Conclusion Diagnosis and management of MIS-A is challenging due to its diverse and infrequent presentation. In this case, MCS was lifesaving and allowed time for recovery and diagnostic assessment.Copyright © 2023 American College of Cardiology Foundation
