T1  - First-in-human phase 1/2 trial of intravenous FBX-101 following hematopoietic stem cell transplantation increases GALC activity, supports brain development, and improves motor function in patients with infantile Krabbe disease: RESKUE clinical trial
T3  - WORLDSymposiumTM 2023. Orlando United States.
A1  - Escolar M.L.
A1  - Lugt M.V.
A1  - Poe M.
A1  - Greco M.
A1  - Werling K.
A1  - De Silva E.
A1  - Ruiz J.
A1  - Szabolcs P.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Infantile Krabbe disease (IKD) is a severe neurodegenerative disorder caused by galactocerebrosidase (GALC) deficiency which leads to death by 2 years. The current standard of care, hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT), improves outcomes in asymptomatic infants, but motor function declines due to peripheral neuropathy. FBX-101 is an intravenously administered AAVrh10-hGALC vector designed to rescue peripheral nerve disease. Nonclinical studies demonstrated that FBX-101 significantly increased survival and corrected peripheral neuropathy in Krabbe mice and dogs when given after HSCT. FBX-101 safety and biodistribution were established in a GLP rat toxicology study. RESKUE is a first-in-human, open-label Phase 1/2 dose-escalating trial to evaluate safety and efficacy of FBX-101 after HSCT for the treatment of patients with IKD. Subjects in cohort 1 receive a single IV infusion of FBX-101 at a low dose (3.0 x 1013 vg/kg) post-HSCT. There is a trial evaluation period of 2 years post administration, with an additional 3 years of follow-up. We report on the safety and preliminary efficacy of the first two patients treated in cohort 1. Subjects received FBX-101 at a dose of 3.0 x 1013 vg/kg, 25 and 29 days after HSCT. FBX-101 was well tolerated, with no treatment-related serious adverse events (including no treatment-related liver enzyme elevations) observed up to Day 270 and Day 120, respectively. No antibodies to AAVrh10 have developed. The subjects have engrafted with full chimerism. We report a significant increase in plasma and CSF GALC enzyme activity following gene transfer, improvement in motor skills measured by PDMS-II, and normal brain white matter growth measured by MRI-DTI. Administration of FBX-101 after HSCT represents a novel gene therapy strategy that leverages the immunosuppressed environment after HSCT, resulting in an immune environment that allows efficient AAV transduction, ensuring that sufficient GALC enzyme is delivered to sustainably support both brain and peripheral nerve development.Copyright © 2022
