T1  - EP081 / #261 PROSPECTIVE OBSERVATIONAL STUDY INTO THE POTENTIAL BENEFITS OF THE DIRECT-TO-IMPLANT TECHNIQUE FOR HIGH FREQUENCY 10 KHZ SPINAL CORD STIMULATION: EPOSTER VIEWING: AS10 - PERIPHERAL NEUROPATHIES
T3  - 3rd Joint Congress of the INS European Chapters. Hamburg Germany.
A1  - Love-Jones S.
A1  - Williams A.
A1  - Clark J.
A1  - King R.
A1  - Chowdhury S.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Introduction: Spinal cord stimulation (SCS) is an established treatment for chronic neuropathic pain1. Evidence suggests forgoing the usual pre-implant trial of SCS by going direct to implant (DTI) can be cost-effective without compromising patient outcomes2. There may be additional patient benefits by reducing radiation exposure, and system benefits by reducing admission spells and surgical time. Here we present evidence from an observational, prospective study of HF10 kHz SCS comparing percutaneous trial versus DTI in patients for the treatment of chronic neuropathic pain of the back and leg. Materials / Methods: Post-market, observational study designed to assess the effectiveness of DTI versus percutaneous trial of HF10 kHz SCS for the treatment of chronic neuropathic pain of the back and leg. Outcome measures included responder rate (RR) defined as percentage of patients achieving >=50% pain relief, self-reported percentage pain relief, Euroqol questionnaire (EQ-5D-5L), radiation exposure and surgical time. Outcomes were measured at 3, 6, and 12 months. Result(s): 17 patients were enrolled (5 male, 12 female) and offered either DTI or percutaneous trial. 8 patients chose DTI and 9 requested a trial of HF10 kHz SCS. Of the 9 trialled patients, 5 proceeded to implant. 3 failed the trial due to lack of efficacy and 1 did not proceed to implant despite a successful trial. Twelve patients were included in the post-implant analysis. The average pain relief at 12months was 56.3%, and the RR was 75% (Figure 1). The average EQ-5D-5L index score improved from a baseline was 0.286 compared to 0.501 at 12months (Figure 2). There were reductions in radiation exposure in the DTI group (381 cGy cm2) versus the trialled groups (1281 cGy cm2). In addition, the average surgical time was 33.29mins in the DTI group and 65.17mins in the trialled groups (Figure 3). Patients who went DTI required 2 fewer hospital spells then those who had trials. One patient had the permanent implant removed due to infection (6 weeks post-implant). Discussion(s): The evidence from this prospective study supports the use of HF10 kHz SCS for treating chronic intractable neuropathic back and/or leg pain. Furthermore, the additional benefits of reduced exposure to radiation and surgical time and previously reported cost savings2 make DTI an interesting option for those practicing SCS. Conclusion(s): Further research is needed to investigate the long-term implications of DTI in a wider clinical/economic context due to the loss of diagnostic utility2 (ability to identify responders) associated with trials of SCS. Learning Objectives: Direct to Implant, Radiation Exposure, Spinal Cord Stimulation Trial, Theater Time.Copyright © 2023
