A1  - Escolar M.L.
A1  - Windreich R.
A1  - Poe M.
A1  - Werling K.
A1  - De Silva E.
A1  - Ruiz J.
A1  - Szabolcs P.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Introduction: Infantile Krabbe (IK) is a neurodegenerative disorder due to decreased galactocylceramidase (GALC) deficiency that leads to death by 2 years of age. Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) improves outcomes but motor function declines due to peripheral neuropathy. FBX-101, is an AAVrh10 vector carrying the GALC gene. In the Twitcher mice and the canine models of KD it has been shown that intravenous (IV) FBX-101 after HSCT is safe and effective. It results in a 8-10 fold synergistic increase in transduction in the CNS and PNS compared to FBX-101 or HSCT alone. Method(s): RESKUE is an open-label, phase 1/2 doseescalating trial evaluating the safety and preliminary efficacy of FBX-101 for transplanted asymptomatic 1 day to 12 months old infants. The primary endpoint is the incidence and severity of adverse and serious adverse events attributed to FBX-101 and HSCT incident of engraftment. Secondary endpoints include evaluations of motor performance and exploratory endpoints of cognition, quality of life, brain DTI, peripheral nerve conduction velocity, ABR, VEP, GALC and psychosine levels. Three subjects get a single IV infusion of FBX-101(3.0x1013 vg/kg) after HSCT, and if safe, 3 will receive (8.0x1013 vg/kg). It is a 2 year study with 3 years of follow-up. Result(s): The first newborn screening subject was diagnosed by deficient GALC, genetic mutations, motor delay and ABRs. HSCT was given at 3.5 months and 3 weeks later IV FBX-101 (3.0x1013 vg/kg) was administered. FBX101 was well tolerated with no treatment-related serious adverse events at Day 90. Engraftment was at the expected time and chimerism was 91%. Motor development was normal and DTI showed normal white matter development, unlike those untreated children with IK. Conclusion(s): The first patient's data of the RESKUE clinical trial shows that IV FBX-101following HSCT has been safe and well tolerated through Day 90 with DTI results suggesting adequate GALC to support normal brain development.
