A1  - Ramirez V.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Background: Cardiac amyloidosis is an increasingly recognized cause of heart failure. Advancements in imaging modalities and treatment options have transformed this once fatal disease into a manageable condition with improved quality of life and prolonged life expectancy. Case: A 62-year-old man with persistent atrial fibrillation, chronic kidney disease and peripheral neuropathy presented with one year of worsening dyspnea with exertion. Symptoms had persisted despite maintaining sinus rhythm. A prior exercise stress test revealed negative perfusion imaging at a submaximal target heart rate. A prior echocardiogram showed a normal ejection fraction of 55% with left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH). On presentation, an electrocardiogram showed normal sinus rhythm with first degree AV block and normal voltage. A cardiac MRI was obtained and revealed increased left ventricular mass and delayed gadolinium enhancement with increased T1 and extracellular volume (ECV), suggestive of amyloidosis. Urine protein electrophoresis showed abnormal light chains with an abnormal kappa/lambda ratio at 0.18. An endomyocardial biopsy revealed infiltration of the interstitium with an amorphous material. Congo red stain was positive. Liquid chromatography with mass spectrometry confirmed AL amyloid deposition. Decision-making: Pursuing a cardiac MRI was demonstrative, as the increased T1 and ECV were suggestive of amyloidosis. In hindsight, there were clinical clues in the history, including chronic kidney disease, peripheral neuropathy, persistent atrial arrhythmias, chronotropic incompetence, AV block and inappropriately normal voltage with LVH, that alluded to amyloidosis. After a year of symptoms, the patient was diagnosed with cardiac AL amyloidosis and started on cyclophosphamide, bortezomib and dexamethasone with an initial decline in kappa/lambda levels. Conclusion(s): Advancements in imaging modalities such as technetium pyrophosphate scan and cardiac MRI can increase the diagnosis of ATTR and AL cardiac amyloidosis. With new treatment options that can halt disease progression, the need for increased clinical suspicion and early diagnosis from clinicians is imperative.Copyright © 2022 American College of Cardiology Foundation
