T1  - MRI-based radiomics and deep learning for pre-operative classification of peripheral nerve sheath tumors
T3  - eEANS2021. Hamburg Germany.
A1  - Serafimova M.
A1  - Shapira Y.
A1  - Artzi M.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Objective: To evaluate the potential of radiomics and deep learning analysis for non-invasive diagnosis and decision making in treatment of benign and malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors (PNST) and further differentiation between various BPNST pathologies based on conventional Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI). Method(s): A retrospective study was performed on one hundred tumors operated between 2010 and 2020 in the Tel Aviv Sourasky Medical Center. Eight of the tumors were of malignant pathology, ninety-two were benign tumors; sixty-two schwannomas, twenty-six neurofibromas and 11 of other pathologies. A computer-based analysis using radiomics and deep learning approaches carried out on post-contrast T1 Weighted MRI images, was performed to differentiate between benign and malignant tumors and between schwannomas and neurofibromas. Model evaluation was performed in a 5-fold cross-validation manner, with calculations based on accuracy, precision, recall and F1 scores. Result(s): Classification results by deep learning outperformed radiomics analysis in both subgroups. For tumor malignancy, the mean overall accuracy across the 5-fold validation sets was 0.95+/-0.03 with deep leaning vs 0.92+/-0.02 with radiomics. F1 scores of BPNST=0.97+/-0.02 vs 0.96+/-0.01, and MPNST=0.77+/-0.13 vs 0 for deep learning vs radiomics analysis, respectively. The mean overall accuracy across the 5-fold validation sets for differentiating between schwannoma and neurofibroma was 0.77+/-0.05 with deep learning vs 0.71+/-0.01 with radiomics. F1 scores for schwannoma = 0.85+/-0.02 vs 0.21+/-0.08, and for neurofibroma = 0.49+/-0.24 vs 31+/-0.81 with deep learning vs radiomics, respectively. Conclusion(s): This study demonstrates the feasibility of using machine-learning analysis, based on conventional MRI, for automatic characterization of PNST malignancy. Deep learning analysis was superior to radiomics in pathology differentiation in all subgroups (BPNST vs MPNST and schwannoma vs neurofibroma). This method may aid in pre-operative decision making and selection of the most appropriate approach for tumor resection.Copyright © 2021
