T1  - Magnetic resonance neurography findings of brachial and lumbosacral plexus in chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy
T3  - Peripheral Nerve Society, PNS 2020. Virtual.
A1  - Nalini A.
A1  - Vengalil S.
A1  - Kulanthaivelu K.
A1  - Nashi S.
A1  - Polavarapu K.
A1  - Preethish-Kumar V.
A1  - Chawla T.
A1  - Shinghavi L.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Introduction: MR Neurography (MRN) is an advanced imaging modality of peripheral nerves, complimenting electrophysiology in diagnosis of various peripheral nerve disorders. We describe the findings of the brachial and lumbosacral root abnormalities by method of assessing the cross sectional area (CSA) and signal intensity (SI) on MRN in CIDP. Method(s): Patients diagnosed to have CIDP underwent MRN on a 3 T Philips scanner. The 3D TSE STIR 3D NerveVIEW sequence parameters were: FOV-300x400x140mm with a matrix of 252x286x127 with reconstructed voxel dimensions of 0.60x0.60x1.1 mm.TR/TI/TE of 2200/280/185 ms. A compressed sense factor of 4 was used for TA of 4 minutes 58 seconds. Result(s): Eleven patients with clinical and electrophysiological features of CIDP showed prominent plexus thickening and hyperintense signals. The M:F = 9:2. Mean age at presentation was 50.2 (range 30-61 years). Mean values of CSA of brachial and lumbosacral roots at different levels are as follows: C5-31.4 mm2, C6-37.0 mm2, C7-37.5 mm2, C8-38.4 mm2, L3-31.1 mm2, L4-31.6 mm2, L5-33.7 mm2, S1-40.1 mm2. CSA of cervical roots ranged from 31.4 to 38.0 mm2 and that of lumbosacral(LS) roots between 31.1 to 40.1 mm2. Mean SI of brachial nerve roots was 4.721 +/- 100 and of lumbosacral nerve roots was 7.373 +/- 200. Though normal CSA for Indian population is lacking, CSA of brachial and LS nerve roots in our series is significantly higher compared to control values described in a previous reports (mean CSA and mean SI of LS roots for controls were 14.91 +/- 2.36 mm2, 1.155 +/- 0.094), mean CSA for control brachial roots was 3.50 +/- 0.62 mm (Japanese patients). Conclusion(s): MR neurography can be a valuable, complimentary tool in the diagnosis of CIDP depicting features of thickening and signal hyperintensities of roots and plexus. Data of control population in Indian scenario and large scale studies may further aid in establishing role of MRN as a potential diagnostic tool in CIDP.
