T1  - Peripheral nerve diffusion tensor imaging as a marker of axonal degeneration in ALS
T3  - 32nd International Symposium on ALS/MND. Virtual.
A1  - Dharmadasa T.
A1  - Simon N.
A1  - Wang C.
A1  - Kiernan M.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Background: Clinical disability is underpinned by lower motor neuron degeneration in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). Methods to measure motor unit loss include assessment of clinical strength, motor nerve conduction, and motor unit number estimation (MUNE) techniques, but these methods have limitations and are challenged by reinnervation, particularly in earlier stages of the disease. Recently, peripheral nerve diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) has been shown to be a reliable means of analysing peripheral nerve injury and degeneration (1,2), but its use as a surrogate marker of axonal loss in ALS remains undetermined. Objective(s): To evaluate the utility of peripheral nerve DTI to assess axonal degeneration as a marker of LMN dysfunction in newly diagnosed ALS patients. Method(s): 30 ALS patients (16 males, 14 females) and 20 healthy controls prospectively underwent 3T MRI sequencing of the forearm, comprising a 30-directional DTI sequence and an axial 2D T1-weighted image to provide correlating anatomical information. Images were analysed using FSL (FMRIB Software Library, Oxford, UK). For measurement of DTI parameters, the median and ulnar nerves were identified at the elbow. Imaging metrics were correlated across clinical (including MRC strength scores), functional (ALSFRS-R) and neurophysiological (MUNE) assessments, while clinical-onset and demographic information were additionally analysed. Result(s): Fractional anisotropy (FA) was significantly reduced at baseline in ALS patients compared to healthy controls across both median and ulnar nerves (p<0.0001) while axial diffusivity was enhanced (p=0.05), suggestive of axonal degeneration. This did not vary according to clinical-onset phenotype. FA from both nerves positively correlated with ALSFRS-R (p<0.001) and MUNE markers (median, p<0.01; ulnar, p=0.02), but there was only a limited relationship with clinical motor strength. No differences of radial diffusivity or mean diffusivity were found in patients compared to controls. Discussion(s): Peripheral DTI is a non-invasive technique that can quantify LMN dysfunction in ALS patients who are in the early stages of their clinically manifest disease. Some DTI metrics (particularly FA) are more sensitive to axonal degeneration in these patients and associate with the level of functional disability and neurophysiological evidence of LMN loss. Further prospective longitudinal analysis in larger cohorts is now needed to evaluate the role of this technique as a sensitive marker of disease progression.
