T1  - Wild-type transthyretin amyloidosis: Clinical, Neurophysiological And Imaging Profile
T3  - Peripheral Nerve Society, PNS 2020. Virtual.
A1  - Campagnolo M.
A1  - Calore C.
A1  - Cacciavillani M.
A1  - Berno T.
A1  - Salvalaggio A.
A1  - Cipriani A.
A1  - Castellani F.
A1  - Briani C.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Introduction: In wild-type transthyretin-related amyloidosis (wt-TTR amyloidosis) cardiac involvement is the predominant feature. The aim of our study was to investigate neurological involvement in a cohort of patients with wt-TTR amyloidosis. Method(s): Twenty-five patients (23 men, mean age 76.6+/-7.8, range 56-86 yrs) underwent EKG and echocardiography. Cardiac MRI and 99mTc-DPD bone scintigraphy were performed in 16/25, tissue biopsies in 6 patients. All the patients underwent neurological examination, 16 also neurophysiology and 12 nerve ultrasound. Result(s): Twelve patients had atrial fibrillation, 6 needed pacemaker/ cardioverter-defibrillator, 2 aortic valve replacement. In 18/25, serum NT-proBNP and troponin were increased. EKG revealed low voltages and repolarization abnormalities in 10/25. Echocardiography disclosed increased thickness of ventricular walls in all the patients, with median ejection fraction 50.5% (range 37-66, reduction in 14/25). Cardiac MRI and bone scintigraphy (16/25) supported the diagnosis. Endomyocardial and salivary glands biopsies (in 5 and one patient, respectively) showed wt-TTR deposits. Sixteen patients had history of carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS), 2 of cubital tunnel syndrome, 2 of lumbar stenosis; 4 had weight loss, 5 autonomic dysfunction. Neurological examination showed weakness and paresthesias at hand muscles in 5/25. Neurophysiology (16/25) disclosed axonal sensory-motor polyneuropathy in 4, mixed demyelinating-axonal features in one, CTS in 8 (6 bilateral), ulnar mononeuropathy in one patient. Nerve ultrasound (12/25) showed median nerve abnormal cross-sectional area (CSA) in 5 patients, with abnormal CSA of both sciatic nerves in one patient with axonal neuropathy, and of the brachial plexus in one patient with mixed neuropathy. Of the 5 patients with neuropathy, 2 had diabetes. Conclusion(s): Neurological involvement other than CTS was found in 18.7% of our patients, both clinically and neurophysiologically. Larger prospective studies are required to define whether wt-TTR amyloidosis might present neurological involvement besides of CTS. The fact that elderly population may present several additional risk factors for neuropathy, should also be considered.
