T1  - Tongue tremor in neurofascin-155 IgG4 seropositive chronic inflammatory polyradiculoneuropathy
T3  - 9th Annual Meeting of the Italian Association for the Study of the Peripheral Nervous System, ASNP 2019. Padua Italy.
A1  - Salvalaggio A.
A1  - Ruiz M.
A1  - Cacciavillani M.
A1  - Callegari I.
A1  - Rinaldi F.
A1  - Campagnolo M.
A1  - Gasparotti R.
A1  - Franciotta D.
A1  - Briani C.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (CIDP) with anti-neurofascin-155 antibodies is a subgroup of CIDP with tremor and poor response to intravenous immunoglobulins. We report on a patient affected with NF-155 IgG4 seropositive CIDP who, along with the typical symptoms of the disease, presented with therapy unresponsive tongue tremor. A previously healthy 23-year-old man came to our attention for a 6 months history of gait difficulties secondary to sensory-motor deficit at lower limbs, and upper limbs tremor severely limiting fine motor tasks. He had ataxic-stepping gait, bilateral distal weakness, tactile hypoesthesia and loss of vibration sense up to the knees, areflexia. High amplitude and low frequency tremor was present at arms and tongue. Besides the tremor, no cerebellar signs were present. Neurophysiology showed signs of diffuse demyelinating neuropathy consistent with CIDP, according to European Federation of Neurological Societies/Peripheral Nerve Society (EFNS/PNS) criteria. Tongue EMG was negative. Nerve ultrasound showed increased cross-sectional area (CSA) of the brachial plexus (156 mm2 on the right side, 129 mm2 on the left side, normal values <= 80 mm2) and nerves trunks. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showed increased total protein level (175 mg/dl) with normal leukocyte count. Laboratory tests were unremarkable, except for serum positivity at high titer (OD 2.9, cutoff 0.2-0.3) for IgG4 antibody to neurofascin-155. Brain and spine MRI with Gadolinium were negative. 3D-MR Neurography revealed bilateral hypertrophy of brachial and lumbosacral plexi. The patient was diagnosed with neurofascin-155 IgG4 seropositive CIDP and treated with intravenous methylprednisolone (1 g/day for 5 days, followed by 1 g/ day for 2 days in the next 2 months) with improvement of motor and sensory deficit, but tremor persisted. Subsequently he underwent intravenous immunoglobulins without improvement of the tremor. Among CIDP, the recently described patients with IgG4 anti-NF-155 antibodies represent a peculiar and distinct subgroup. Latest reports, as the present, are still enriching the definition of this CIDP subgroup with younger age, severe weakness, predominantly distal sensory disturbances, cranial nerve hypertrophy, intention tremor, sensory ataxia and poor response to intravenous immunoglobulins. NF-155 antibodies have been shown also to stain rat cerebellum suggesting a possible central origin of the therapy unresponsive tremor, and in some patients central nervous system demyelinating alterations have also been detected at the brain MRI. Besides one patient with immunotherapy-responsive head and voice tremor, this is to our knowledge the first NF-155 antibody seropositive patient with arms and tongue tremor, who is now candidate to rituximab treatment.
