A1  - Marchioni E.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Background and aims: The genetic component of multiple sclerosis (MS) is now set to 201 common variants and 32 signals from HLA. It is unclear how genetic knowledge can be used in clinical practice, for example in differential diagnosis between MS and other inflammatory conditions with different prognosis like adult-onset post-infectious inflammatory syndromes (APIS) Methods: 96 incident APIS have been consecutively recruited (1996-2016) at Mondino Institute, diagnosed according to clinical, MRI and CSF findings and followedup for >18 months. Patients have been typed using Illumina array, and genotypes imputed using the 1000 Genomes Project reference panel. A weighted genetic risk score (wGRS) has been calculated based on known MS loci Results: Diagnosis includes encephalomyelitis (44%), myelitis (44%) and encephalitis (12%), and in 32% peripheral nervous system (PNS) was involved. Mean age of onset was 50 years, and female:male ratio 1.38. Patients were followed-up for 7 years and, at last follow-up, disease was monophasic in 67% and 54% had a low disability grade. The wGRS score of APIS cases was comparable to 370 healthy controls (wGRS=20.9 vs 20.7), while significantly lower compared to 907 RRMS cases (wGRS=21.2; p<0.0001). The separation was even stronger for APIS with PNS involvement vs RRMS (wGRS=20.6; p<0.0001) Conclusion(s): The distinction between MS and APIS, including ADEM, myelitis and encephalitis, is still hard to make. Our study shows that the new set of MS risk alleles does not confer increased susceptibility to APIS, and it supports the importance to discriminate these cases from MS.
