T1  - Non-ischemic myocardial lesions in type 2 diabetes mellitus as assessed by late gadolinium enhancement magnetic resonance imaging: A cohort study of lesion types and prevalence
T3  - European Society of Cardiology Congress, ESC 2018. Munich Germany.
A1  - Bojer A.S.
A1  - Heyn Soerensen M.
A1  - Gaede P.
A1  - Madsen P.L.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Background: Abundant data testify to a strong association between diabetes and heart failure from non-ischemic cardiomyopathy ("diabetic cardiomyopathy") but its cause and development is not well accounted for. Autopsy studies have reported fibrosis including confluent scarring unrelated to ischemic heart disease (IHD). In patients with diabetes, previous magnetic resonance imaging studies with late-gadolinium enhancement (LGE) have aimed at ischemic LGE changes, but to the best of our knowledge no larger cohort study has so far quantified non-ischaemic changes. Purpose(s): To characterize localized LGE with special emphasis on non-ischemic lesions in a broad population of patients with type 2 diabetes DM2 using LGE cardiac magnetic resonance imaging. Method(s): A cross-sectional study of 268 patients with DM2 was conducted at the out-patient clinic of our Hospital. 25 age matched healthy controls. Participants were studied with echocardiography and cardiac magnetic resonance with LGE. All regions were verified by at least one other orthogonal plane. Two physicians analyzed the presence of LGE, a third reviewer settled disagreements. Result(s): All controls had analyzable scans without LGE. In patients with diabetes, 227 LGE scans were analyzable. Of these 49 (21.6%) had LGE changes: 26 had ischemic LGE changes (subendocardial or transmural and consistent with a coronary artery perfusion territory); 22 patients had non-ischemic LGE changes; and 1 patient had both ischemic and non-ischemic LGE changes. In 21/23 patients with non-ischemic changes, these appeared rounded, 0.5x1.5 cm, and located mid-wall in the basal and infero-lateral part of the left ventricle (LV) myocardium. 1 patient had LGE changes in the papillary muscles, and 1 patient had LGE changes located in the basal part of the inter-ventricular septum. In univariate analysis, patients with non-ischemic LGE changes were twice as likely to suffer from retinopathy (55% vs. 25%, p=0.004) with a tendency for association to peripheral neuropathy (P=0.0730) than patients without LGE lesions. Further, patients with non-ischemic LGE lesions had larger left ventricle mass (150 (95% CI 131-169) g vs. 132 (95% CI 127-137) g, p=0.022), larger left atrial maximal volume (103 (92-115) mL vs. 90 (87-93) mL, p=0.013), and higher E/e* on echocardiography (8.6 (7.1-10.3) vs. 7.3 (7.0-7.7), p=0.048) than patients without LGE. There was no difference in albuminuria, duration of diabetes or other risk factors of IHD. Conclusion(s): In patients with type 2 diabetes nearly half of identified LGE changes were not related to IHD. Identified non-ischemic changes followed a similar pattern of rounded infero-lateral lesions and were related to higher LV mass and diastolic dysfunction as well as signs of poor long-term glycemic control. We speculate the described LGE findings may be related to previous fibrosis described in autopsy studies, and hence could be specific for diabetes cardiomyopathy.
