N2  - Introduction: the relationship between Multiple Sclerosis (MS) and other acquired demyelinating syndromes (ADS), including acute demyelinating encephalomyelitis (ADEM), transverse myelitis and encephalitis, is still unclear especially when they occur in adult age. A previous study, performed on 188 pediatric cases of ADS, demonstrated that MS genetic risk loci increase the risk of pediatric-onset MS, but not of other ADS. Aim(s): to evaluate the cumulative contribution of the new set of >200 MS risk variants in the development of adult cases of ADS. Method(s): 99 incident adult-onset ADS cases have been consecutively recruited between 1996 and 2016 at National Neurological Institute of Pavia. Patients were diagnosed according to clinical, MRI and CSF findings and followed-up for a minimum of 18 months. Seven of them received other diagnoses over time: 4 converted to MS, 2 received a diagnosis of neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorders (NMOSD) with AQP4 positivity and 1 with HSV1-related infective encephalitis. Patients have been typed using Illumina Infinium OmniExpress BeadChip and genotypes imputed using the 1000 Genomes phase 3 ALL panel. A weighted genetic risk score (wGRS) has been calculated based on 201 MS loci, 2 additional MS SNPs emerged from the ExomeChip study and the HLA DRB1*15:01 tagging SNP rs3135388. Result(s): After quality control, 89 ADS cases, with diagnoses encompassing encephalomyelitis (n=39), myelitis (n=39) and encephalitis (n=11), were analyzed. Contemporary involvement of peripheral nervous system was detected in 31 patients. Mean age of onset was 50 years, and female:male ratio was 1.49. Patients were followed up for 7.2+/-4.2 years and, at last follow-up, 60 of them had a low disability grade (modified Rankin scale < = 2). The wGRS score of adult ADS cases was comparable to the one of 370 healthy controls (HC) (mean value=20.81 vs 20.99; p=NS), while it was significantly lower compared to the wGRS of 462 relapsing remitting MS cases (mean value=21.49; p< 0.0001). Also in terms of calculation of the Area Under the receiver operating characteristics Curve (AUC), the wGRS score did not allow to discriminate between ADS and HC, differently from MS (AUC=0.56 vs 0.697 respectively). Conclusion(s): Our study shows that the new set of MS risk alleles do not confer increased susceptibility to adult-onset ADS. Despite the small sample size, this study supports the need to consider and discriminate ADS diagnosis from MS also in adult patients.
