T1  - Impaired haemodynamic response to thermal pain in patients with painful diabetic neuropathy: A dynamic susceptibility contrast magnetic resonance imaging study
T3  - 2018 Diabetes UK Professional Conference. London United Kingdom.
A1  - Greig M.
A1  - Wilkinson I.D.
A1  - Pallai S.
A1  - Gandhi R.
A1  - Selvarajah D.
A1  - Tesfaye S.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Aim: Painful diabetic peripheral neuropathy (P-DPN) causes distressing neuropathic pain that is partially responsive to treatment. Understanding central nervous system correlates of P-DPN is vital to develop effective therapeutics. The aim of this study was to measure cerebral perfusion of pain processing areas of the brain using Magnetic resonance-Dynamic susceptibility Contrast (MR-DSC) imaging at rest and during experimental pain. Method(s): Fifty-four subjects with Type 1 diabetes [19 P-DPN, 23 painless DPN, 13 Type 1 diabetes subjects without DPN and 19 healthy volunteers (HV)] underwent MR-DSC with intravenous gadolinium-chelate contrast. Subjects were scanned at baseline and during 90s of heat-pain applied to the sensate right lateral thigh. Time-to-peak (TTP) concentration of gadolinium and DTTP was measured in the right and left thalamus (Rthal, Lthal) and right and left sensory cortex (RSC, LSC). Result(s): At baseline, mean TTP (seconds) was not statistically significant different between groups. DTTP in response to thermal pain was significantly different between the groups at the Lthal (p = 0.021), Rthal (p = 0.003), LSC (p = 0.009) and RSC (p = 0.008). There was a significant interaction effect between group and pain intensity. Plots of these interactions suggest DTTP is positively correlated to pain intensity score for P-DPN group but negatively correlated for controls. Conclusion(s): TTP measurements during an experimental painful stimulus show intriguing differences of perfusion between groups according to pain perception, which may be due to top down processing. This novel finding may serve as an objective marker of P-DPN, and in the future may facilitate the development of novel treatments.
