T1  - Osteomyelitis of the foot in a recently diagnosed type 2 diabetic with well controlled HbA1c
T3  - 2018 RAMI Intern Section Meeting. Dublin Ireland.
A1  - Robinson A.
A1  - McGimsey T.
A1  - Divilly P.
A1  - Pazderska A.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Introduction: Through a combination of neuropathy and poor vascular perfusion, diabetic foot ulcers are a common complication of patients with established diabetes. Good glycaemic control has been demonstrated to reduce the risk of ulcers and infections [1] Case Presentation: A 63-year old man presented to hospital with an oedematous and erythematous right foot, as well as an ulcer on the base of the fifth digit, on a background of type 2 diabetes diagnosed 2 years previously. Despite his relatively new diagnosis and his well-controlled HbA1c (39mmol/mol on admission) he had pronounced sensory neuropathy bilaterally in his lower limbs.MRI of his foot revealed osteomyelitis. He also had impalpable pulses. Vascular Ultrasound with Ankle-Brachial Pressure Index (ABPI) demonstrated severely calcified vessels in both legs. He was found to have a vitamin B12 deficiency, which can be associated with peripheral neuropathy. Vitamin B12 deficiency is a known side effect of Metformin, a drug which he had been taking since his diagnosis. Nerve conduction studies demonstrated a large fibre sensory axonal neuropathy consistent with diabetic neuropathy. Although rare, it has been recognised that neuropathy can precede the diagnosis of diabetes by many years [2]. He was initiated on IV antibiotics and, following clinical improvement, was discharged on outpatient parenteral antibiotic therapy (OPAT). Discussion(s): Diabetic foot ulcers are an important and serious complication of diabetes. While they typically occur in patients with longestablished disease, it is important to remain vigilant for other causes of neuropathy.
