Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Objective: To present an image panel of peripheral nerve impairment in infectious peripheral neuropathies. Background(s): Assessment of peripheral nerves with imaging methods has been increasingly used in the diagnostic approach and follow-up of patients with peripheral neuropathy. There are many types of techniques that can be used to delimitate and study nerves pathways, shape and thickness. High-resolution Ultrasound (US) with a computerized assessment include techniques such as cross sectional area, cross sectional area variability along the anatomical course, echogenicity, vascularity and mobility of the peripheral nerves allowing detailed structural images. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) had a limited use because of the difficulty in delineating nerves, but now, with special software and tractography (DTI), neural tracts can be studied through three-dimensional visualization of nerve tissue. Design/Methods: We conducted a review of MRI, DTI tractography and USG images of patients with Guillain- Barre syndrome (GBS) associated with Zika virus infection and leprosy. Findings were correlated with clinical history, physical examination, electrophysiological study and nerve biopsy. Result(s): DTI tractography can be very useful in the context of peripheral neuropathies as we observed; it is a noninvasive, safe and technically reproducible technology with high sensitivity for evaluation of peripheral nerves. USG is a useful bedside exam is particularly well-suited to evaluate the nerves of an entire extremity or bilateral extremities in one examination, in the evaluation of peripheral nerve disease, such as acute infectious polyneuropathy. If done with the proper technique it can reliably show enlarged sectional areas of nerves. Conclusion(s): Peripheral nerve evaluation with these imaging methods seems to be useful and promising, mostly with US. Expanding their usage should be advocated as well as studies associating imaging and neurophysiological studies of peripheral nerves in infectious diseases.
