T1  - Multinuclear MR imaging in diabetic peripheral neuropathy
T3  - 2018 Peripheral Nerve Society Annual Meeting. Baltimore, MD United States.
A1  - Parasoglou P.
A1  - Baete S.
A1  - Ho A.
A1  - Brown R.
A1  - Convit A.
A1  - Garwood E.
A1  - Mroczek K.
A1  - Slade J.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN) is characterized by impairments in metabolic and microvascular (MV) functions,1 which damage the endoneural capillaries that supply the peripheral nerves.2 Prolonged DPN can lead to local ischemic conditions in the lower extremities, causing loss of peripheral nerve integrity, neurogenic muscle atrophy, fatty infiltration, and loss of endurance.3 We have implemented novel multinuclear MRI techniques that can reliably assess skeletal muscle metabolic function, as well as MV function, levels of intramuscular adipose tissue (IMAT), and peripheral nerve structural integrity, which together can provide mechanistic insight into adaptations that accompany DPN (4-6). We acquired multinuclear MRI data on eight patients with DPN and seven healthy controls. All participants were scanned on a Siemens 3 T whole body MRI scanner (Siemens, Healthineers, Erlangen, Germany) using a home-built dual-tuned (31P/1H) knee coil array. We used a rapid phosphorus (31P)-MRI technique to assess lower leg muscle mitochondrial function; Blood Oxygen Level Dependent (BOLD-MRI) to evaluated lower leg MV function, IDEAL MRI to quantify levels of IMAT, and diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) to assess tibial, deep fibular, and superficial fibular nerve structural integrity. Patients with DPN showed distinct patterns of increased IMAT levels compared to controls, as well as prolonged phosphocreatine resynthesis rates following 90-s plantar flexion using an MRI compatible ergometer. Lower leg muscle MV function was reduced in DPN as measured with BOLD-MRI following single maximum voluntary contractions. Further, levels of fractional anisotropy in all three nerves examined were lower in DPN suggesting loss of nerve integrity. Advanced multinuclear-MRI can provide mechanistic insight into adaptations that accompany DPN. These include changes in IMAT, MV and metabolic functions of the skeletal muscle, as well as peripheral nerve function and integrity. Understanding these adaptations may help better predict patient outcomes, and develop more effective treatments for DPN.
