T1  - Usefulness of neurographic dwi and dti based tecnhiques for the evaluation of peripheral nerve tumors
T3  - 15th Neuroradiology Congress. Valencia Spain.
A1  - Noguerol T.M.
A1  - Barbero J.P.M.
A1  - Cabrera M.G.
A1  - Hurtado P.M.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Introduction - Review the physical basis and technical specifications for the acquisition of DWI-Neurography and DTI-Neurography studies. - Show the characteristics of peripheral nerves tumors using these techniques. Material and Methods Description of the basic sequence design and technical parameters required for the acquisition of DWI and DTI-Neurography studies. Bibliographic update of the main applications of DWI and DTINeurography for assessing peripheral nerve tumors. Illustrate, by own examples, the usefulness and limitations of DWI and DTI-Neurography studies for the evaluation of this kind of lesions. Results We present examples of benign lesions (Schwannoma and Neurofibroma), malignant lesions (Neurofibrosacroma) and pseudotumoral lesions such as neuromas or intraneural ganglion cysts. The characteristic sof theselesions, both in DWI and DTI, is detailed according to the degree of restriction of diffusion (signal intensity on DWI and ADC values) and their behavior in the study of DTI (FAvalues and relationship with the rest of the nerve in the 3D neurographic reconstructions (tractography). The added value of these techniques over conventional sequences is discussed and the possible inclusion or not as a part of routine MRI protocols for assessing tumoral lesions of peripheral nerves. Conclusions DWI and DTI Neurography studies allow to improve characterization of peripheral nerve tumors from bothmorphologically (using 3D reconstructions), and functional point of view, using parameters derived from their acquisitions (ADC, FA). This manner, differential diagnosis between them can be facilitated increasing our overall diagnostic accuracy. These techniques are showing promising results and may be considered for inclusion as part of routine protocols for assessing peripheral nerve tumors.
