T1  - Simultaneously excited multi-slice DTI in the peripheral nerves
T3  - 2017 Annual Meeting of the Orthopaedic Research Society. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Jeon T.
A1  - Tan E.T.
A1  - Fung M.M.K.
A1  - Sneag D.B.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - In this study we investigated the feasibility of using simultaneously excited multi-slice echo planar imaging (EPI) acquired using diffusion magnetic resonance imaging (dMRI) to delineate the peripheral nerves. Multiband (MB) EPI can excite and refocus multiple slice locations simultaneously using multiband radiofrequency excitation and refocusing pulses which can be un-aliased by exploiting the differences in coil sensitivities. We hypothesize that MB diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) provides excellent signal to noise ratio with the added incentive of faster scan time compared to conventional DTI. Here we aim to focus on interrogating the median nerve at the level of the elbow with MB DTI EPI using quantitative measurements of the DTI metrics. Method(s): Subjects and data acquisition: Three consented healthy volunteers were scanned at the level of the elbow on a GE 3T 60cm bore MR scanner (MR 750) using a 16 channel flexible extremity array in the superman position with MB DTI and conventional DTI. The DTI parameters for the MB are as follows: FOV: 12cmx12cm, Matrix: 128x128mm, TR/TE: 2000/57.2ms, BW: 195kHz, slice thickness: 3mm, b=0,600s/mm2, 16 gradient directions, 4 NEX, for a total scan time of 2minutes and 23 seconds. The standard DTI protocol is as follows: FOV: 12cmx12cm, Matrix: 80x40mm, TR/TE: 3500/66.1ms, BW: 167kHz, slice thickness: 3mm, b=0,600s/mm2, 16 gradient directions, 4 NEX, for a total scan time of 3minutes and 48 seconds. Tensor fitting and segmentation: The tensor fitting was conducted with DWI of b=600s/mm2 to obtain the DTI maps using DTI Studio (mristudio.org). The median nerve was extracted based on the contrast of the DTI color map using a regional growing algorithm, e.g. Fig. 1, by thresholding the FA values between 0.3 and 0.8. The fractional anisotropy (FA), mean diffusivity (MD), axial diffusivity (AD), and radial diffusivity (RD) were measured using the median nerve as a mask. Result(s): As shown in Fig. 2, the MB DTI shows improved SNR and delineation of anatomic structures compared to conventional DTI. For the MB DTI, the mean FA (0.52+/-0.04), AD (2.8x10-3mm+/-0.22), RD (1.27x10-3mm+/-0.2), MD (5.02x10-3mm2/s+/-0.94) were similar to conventional DTI mean FA (0.47+/-0.08), AD (2.21x10-3mm+/-0.18), RD (1.0 x10-3mm+/-0.0), MD (4.22x10-3mm2/s+/-0.37). There were no significant differences between MB DTI and conventional DTI for all measurements using ANOVA (p=0.42). Discussion(s): MB DTI reduces scan time with improved image quality by visual inspection compared to conventional DTI. The DTI metrics were consistent between both MB and non-MB DTI for the median nerve. Many researchers have used MB DTI in the brain, however, to our best knowledge, this is the first time that MB DTI has been reported to delineate the median nerve. (Figure Presented).
