A1  - Johnson K.
A1  - Schwenk M.
A1  - Najafi B.
A1  - Taljanovic M.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Purpose: Chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy (CIPN) is a common condition seen in up to 75% of individuals undergoing chemotherapy, manifesting as pain and numbness of the extremities leading to functional disability and diminished quality of life. CIPN has been related to direct and indirect physiologic effects of anti-cancer agents on peripheral nerves. Current methods of measurement of CIPN include subjective measures, clinical measures, and invasive electrodiagnostic testing which have many pitfalls. MR neurography (MRN) and diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) show promise in the workup of peripheral nerves. These techniques, not previously studied in CIPN, have promise as a biomarker of onset and severity of CIPN and an aid to evaluating preventive strategies. In addition, these techniques have the potential of greatly impacting diagnosis, outcome, and outlook for these patients. We investigated a possible relationship between DTI and CIPN. Material(s) and Method(s): Cancer patients on chemotherapy with and without subjective CIPN of the feet were evaluated using vibratory perception threshold (VPT) testing. A VPT score of >=25Volts defined the presence of CIPN. The posterior tibial nerve and branches in both feet were imaged using MRN and DTI. Fractional anisotropy (FA) and apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) values were measured at the posterior tibial, medial plantar and lateral plantar nerves. Measurements for the CIPN group were compared to measurements in the group without CIPN by VPT cut-off. Correlations and possible relationships between DTI parameters and CIPN were analyzed. Result(s): A total of nine patients were enrolled; a total of 15 feet were imaged (8 feet with CIPN, 7 feet without CIPN). The average age was 60.6 +/- 13.4 years (range = 33-74). The posterior tibial nerve ADC values were significantly lower than the medial plantar nerve ADC values in all feet (F = 3.50, p = 0.04). We found a correlation with FA and ADC values at specific nerve locations with CIPN, with the left medial plantar nerve FA value and left lateral plantar nerve ADC value demonstrating the strongest positive correlations (0.73 and 0.62, respectively). Conclusion(s): This pilot study provides preliminary data showing correlations between FA and ADC measurements with CIPN. MRN with DTI shows promising results as a potential objective biomarker for CIPN and may provide helpful information for patients with cancer on chemotherapy and with peripheral neuropathy. A larger, more definitive study of MRN with DTI as an objective biomarker and possible early predictor for CIPN is warranted.
