N2  - Objective: To evaluate the utility of magnetic resonance neurography (MRN) for the detection of neuropathy in Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 1A (CMT1A). Background(s): Charcot-Marie-Tooth (CMT) is diagnosed based on clinical examination, nerve conduction studies (NCS) and genetic analysis. NCS is the golden standard for evaluation of peripheral neuropathies, however, the nerves may be inexcitable and the degree of severity is difficult to assess more precisely. These factors underscore the need for sensitive methods to determine the presence and severity of neuropathy in CMT. Design/Methods: MRN was performed in proximal and distal nerve segments of the lower extremity (sciatic and tibial nerve) in 15 patients with CMT1A and 30 healthy controls (HC). MRN consisted of conventional MR imaging to calculate T2-relaxation-time (T2) and proton-spin-density (PD) and diffusion-tensor-imaging (DTI) to determine fractional-anisotropy (FA) and the apparent-diffusion-coefficient (ADC). The findings at MRN were related to neuropathy severity based on clinical examinations and nerve conduction studies. Sensitivity and specificity of MRN was estimated from receiver operating characteristics (ROC). Result(s): In CMT1A patients, as compared to HC, DTI of the sciatic nerve showed lower FA values (HC: 0.49+/-0.06; CMT1A: 0.36+/-0.07) (p<0.01) and higher ADC values (HC: 1.42+/-0.12 x 10-3mm2/s; CMT1A: 1.67+/-0.20 x 10-3mm2/s) (p< 0.01). Similar findings were seen for the tibial nerve with lower FA values (p<0.01) and higher ADC values (p<0.01). Conventional T2 showed no difference, however, the PD of the sciatic nerve was higher in the patient group (HC: 381+/-80; CMT1A: 545+/-101) (p<0.01). There were close associations between neuropathy severity and MRN with the closest correlation between FA and nerve conduction velocity in the sciatic nerve (R2=0.58; p<0.01). ROC analyses showed good separation of CMT1A and HC. Conclusion(s): MRN examination enables detection of neuropathic abnormalities in patients with CMT1A and may prove to be a responsive measure for monitoring of the neuropathy.
