T1  - Magnetic resonance imaging of nerves around metallic hardware: Challenges, pitfalls, and optimization techniques
T3  - 40th Annual Scientific Meeting of the Society of Skeletal Radiology. Santa Barbara, CA United States.
A1  - Kalia V.
A1  - Yeong L.
A1  - Carrino J.
A1  - Sneag D.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Purpose: MRI of peripheral nerves around metallic hardware presents unique technical and methodologic challenges. The information gleaned from such imaging, however, is sometimes invaluable in clinical management of peripheral nerve injuries. The purpose of this study was to retrospectively review a collection of recent clinical cases involving MRI of peripheral nerves around metal and to discuss the challenges, pitfalls, and current optimization techniques for such imaging. Material(s) and Method(s): IRB approval was obtained for this retrospective analysis. A search of the radiology information system was performed to identify a subset of clinical cases which highlight advances in peripheral nerve imaging around metal. Cases from our institutional peripheral nerve imaging program were selected based on the following criteria: (1) metallic hardware present nearby peripheral nerves of interest, and (2) imaging with metal artifact reduction sequences (MARS) and techniques employed. Examples of techniques employed on MRI included: careful selection of field of view, matrix and slice thickness to optimize spatial resolution, increased bandwidth to decrease distortion from metal, use of planes orthogonal to the nerve's longitudinal axis, use of 3.0 Tesla when possible given inherent increased signal to noise ratio and faster acquisition time, and others. Select patients' MR images with and without MARS were analyzed for peripheral nerve visibility, conspicuity, and for detailed characterization of nerve abnormalities, and imaging findings were correlated with medical histories. Result(s): Thirty clinical cases of peripheral nerve imaging around metallic hardware were selected as part of this work. Imaged nerves included: radial (n = 5), musculocutaneous (n = 2), median (n = 4), suprascapular (n = 3), axillary (n = 2), sciatic (n = 7), common peroneal (n = 2), tibial (n = 2), and femoral (n = 3). In several of the analyzed cases, the suspected clinical diagnosis could not be adequately diagnosed on the images acquired without the optimized metal-artifact reduction sequence and techniques. Furthermore, in a few cases, the obscured imaging findings were critical to immediate patient management. After assessing dozens of clinical cases, we found the optimal fat suppression technique for adequately visualizing nerves in the presence of metal to be a Dixon pulse sequence, which inherently provides greater SNR as compared to a STIR sequence. Dixon is also less susceptible to field inhomogeneity compared to adiabatic suppression techniques, which is particularly important when imaging the extremity, off iso-center, and large field of views such as the brachial and lumbosacral plexi. However, in cases where the nerve of interest is immediately adjacent to metallic hardware, artifact on the Dixon sequence may obscure regional anatomy, and an inversion recovery sequence may be preferred. Conclusion(s): Optimal imaging of peripheral nerves around metallic hardware is challenging but can be invaluable to patient management. Here, we identified clinical scenarios where peripheral nerves can be imaged in the presence of large metallic prostheses or when other metallic hardware obscures visualization, such as distal radius fracture fixation hardware obscuring nerves about the wrist. MRI's major advantage is its ability to directly visualize nerves in multiple planes with high spatial and contrast resolution, which affords it the ability to define cause, localize site, and grade the severity of peripheral nerve injury.
