N2  - Background: Painful diabetic peripheral neuropathy(DPN) is the most common cause of chronic neuropathic pain, although the evidence of the central nervous system involvement has been scarce. Objective(s): The aim of the present study was to investigate the differences of regional grey matter volume in subjects with painful DPN. Patients and Methods/Material and Methods: Fifteen subjects with painful DPN(62.6+/-8.1 years old, 5 females, HbA1c 6.96+/-1.10%) and nineteen healthy volunteer(66.2+/-10.0 years old, 12 females) underwent three-dimensional T1-weighted brain magnetic resonance imaging at 3.0 tesla. We conducted Voxel-based morphometry by using statistical parametric mapping(SPM)12's segmentation and Dartel normalization. The initial voxel threshold was set to .001 uncorrected. Clusters were considered as significant when falling below a cluster corrected p = 0.05(FWE). Result(s): Compared to the control group, painful DPN group demonstrated decreased grey matter volume in the medial prefrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, putamen, in addition to increased grey matter volume in primary sensorimotor area. Conclusion(s): Painful DPN may affect the pain-matrix which alter the regional brain volume and may explain the pathophysiology of chronic pain in patients with neuropathy.
