T1  - Ultrasound application from CIP to MND
T3  - 22nd Meeting of the European Society of Neurosonology and Cerebral Hemodynamics, ESNCH 2017. Berlin Germany.
A1  - Van Alfen N.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Neuromuscular ultrasound is a patient-friendly, inexpensive, point-ofcare technique with proven sensitivity and validity to help diagnose patients with a neuromuscular disorder. It is not a replacement for EMG, buth rather, as EMG is a measurement of function and ultrasound is concerned with structure, the use of both techniques is complementary; quite similar to combining EEG and MRI for CNS disorders. Nerve and muscle ultrasound can be used for screening for the presence of a neuromuscular disorder, diagnosing entrapment, tumours, inflammatory neuropathies and motor neuron disease, and is helpful as an non-invasive parameter for the follow up of patients with muscular dystrophy and assessment of respiratory muscle function. US also allows for dynamic imaging of moving muscle tissue, and can for example screen for fasciculations with high diagnostic certainty. Muscle ultrasound has a sensitivity for detecting neuromuscular disorders of 67%-86% using visual evaluation alone, and a diagnostic sensitivity for the presence of a neuromuscular disorder of over 90% using quantified analysis of grayscale values or backscatter Ultrasound scanning of peripheral nerves measures nerve size as the cross-sectional area (CSA), which can be used for determining pathology when population reference values are taken into account. Nerve ultrasound has excellent diagnostic values, and is more sensitive than MRI (93% vs 67%), with equivalent specificity (86%).
