N2  - Background and aims: Transthyretin (TTR) amyloidosis is a devastating autosomal dominantly inherited disorder with familial amyloidotic polyneuropathy (FAP) as one of its main features. We systematically studied FAP using a high-resolution ultrasonography (US). Method(s): In this prospective cross-sectional study we took structured patients' history, performed neurological examination, and measured peripheral nerve cross sectional areas (CSAs) using US. Nerve CSAs were bilaterally measured at 14 standard locations. Demographic and US findings were compared to unilateral findings obtained in 50 healthy volunteers. Result(s): Study included 33 FAP patients and 21 asymptomatic TTR gene mutation carriers. Patients' peripheral nerve CSAs were significantly larger than controls', most dramatically at the common entrapment sites (e.g., median nerve at the wrist and ulnar nerve at the elbow), and in the proximal nerve segments (e.g., median nerve in the upper arm; Fig. 1). Findings in carriers were similar but less pronounced, with CSAs being significantly larger compared to the control group only in the median nerve at the upper arm. Conclusion(s): We confirmed peripheral nerve thickening in FAP patients, which is probably caused by amyloid accumulation with resulting nerve oedema and fibrosis. As previously demonstrated in patho-histological and magnetic resonance neurography studies nerve thickenings were most pronounced in the proximal nerve segments. Similar findings in diabetic symmetric distal and vasculitic polyneuropathies were attributed to the ischaemic nerve damage at the watershed zones of poor perfusion, such as in the mid-upper arm or the mid-thigh. Our data therefore support microvascular impairment in the pathogenesis of FAP. (Figure Presented).
