Y1  - 2016//
N2  - MR imaging is currently the premier method of depicting disorders of the peripheral nervous system. The information obtained with MR imaging may be complementary to the information provided with ultrasound, electromyography and nerve conduction studies in cases of nerve entrapment. Pathology of the peripheral nerves can be detected and characterized with MR imaging by recognizing changes in the morphology and signal intensity of abnormal nerves. MR imaging can also depict the changes that occur in muscles related to denervation. Entrapment of a peripheral nerve causes signal alteration within the muscles innervated by that nerve on MR images. These signal abnormalities seen with MR imaging may then be followed to resolution or progressive atrophy and fatty infiltration. Moreover, the site and cause of entrapment may be discovered with MR imaging by following the nerve implicated from the distribution of abnormal muscles on MR imaging. These features of MR imaging may help establish the cause of pain and dysfunction related to pathology of the peripheral nervous system. For routine imaging of peripheral nerves on high field systems we use high-resolution fast-spin-echo proton density images (TR 3000/ TE 35) coupled with matching fat-suppressed fast-spin-echo T2- weighted images (TR 4000/TE 50) at the same slice locations in all three planes of section. We also use this method routinely when evaluating joints for internal derangement. Entrapment neuropathies and injuries of peripheral nerves: Morphologic findings on MR imaging in cases of peripheral nerve injury include discontinuity of the nerve and a lax "coiled spring" appearance in cases of nerve transection. An amputation neuroma is characterized by enlargement of the transected nerve at its termination. A peripheral nerve may be visibly compressed or flattened on MR imaging at a site of entrapment or nerve subluxation. Enlargement of a peripheral nerve may be seen proximal or distal to a site of compression. Increased signal on T2-weighted images is the initial MR imaging finding within muscles that are undergoing denervation. Although this increase in relative signal intensity has been reported as early as 24 h after a severe nerve injury, it is typically seen after two to three weeks of denervation on STIR or fat-suppressed T2-weighted images. There is typically perifascial edema present in muscle strain injury that is absent in subacute denervation that allows differentiation of these two entities on MR imaging. In subacute denervation the affected muscles are relatively bright on T2-weighted images secondary to muscle fiber shrinkage and associated increases in extracellular water. These changes may be followed to resolution or may be followed to progressive atrophy and fatty infiltration. The distribution of abnormal muscles on MR imaging may lead to a deduction about the specific nerve that is injured and prompt a careful search along the course of that nerve for a mass. The brachial plexus and lumbosacral plexus: Primary abnormalities of the plexus are less common than spinal disorders. We most commonly image the brachial plexus or lumbosacral plexus after an unrevealing MR study of the cervical spine or lumbar spine. The brachial plexus and lumbosacral plexus are best imaged, like the spine and joints, with specialized phased array surface coils. These studies require good technique to achieve homogeneous fat suppression and adequate resolution. Despite a more complex wiring diagram compared with the spine and extremities, pathology of the plexus is just as well seen with MR imaging. Commonly encountered entities in both regions include entrapment related to tumors, hematomas, aneurysms, abscesses, and adenopathy as well as dysfunction related to neuritis and radiation therapy. Trauma is more frequent in the brachial plexus relative to the lumbar plexus and sacral plexus. Nerve root avulsions in the cervical spine are associated with a dural tear and traumatic pseudomeningocele that can be seen with MR imaging. Myelography with computed tomography of the cervical spine may provide complementary information in these cases. MR imaging of the sacral plexus and the sciatic nerve can reveal pathoanatomic changes in patients that present with pyriformis syndrome. The upper extremity: MR imaging can be used to evaluate upper extremity nerves after blunt or penetrating trauma. MR imaging is also commonly performed to exclude a mass along the course of a given nerve in patients with a specific neuropathy. In the shoulder, we commonly find unsuspected entrapment of the suprascapular nerve secondary to a paralabral cyst associated with a superior labral tear. Entrapment of the axillary nerve within the quadrilateral space from a paralabral cyst associated with an inferior labral tear may also be seen, but is much less common. In the elbow, we commonly see increased signal within the ulnar nerve in patients with cubital tunnel syndrome. Spurs that undermine the cubital tunnel as well as an anomalous anconeous epitrochlearis muscle superficial to the ulnar nerve are well seen with MR imaging. Subluxation of the ulnar nerve may be seen on scans performed in flexion in patients with an absent or insufficient cubital tunnel retinaculum. We occasionally see entrapment of the deep and superficial branches of the radial nerve in patients with enlargement of the bicipital radial bursa that may present as a painful antecubital fossa mass or may be clinically occult. Entrapment of the median nerve from enlargement of the interosseous bursa is much less commonly encountered. In the wrist, we commonly see increased signal within the median nerve in patients with carpal tunnel syndrome. Entrapment of the ulnar nerve in Guyon's canal is less commonly encountered. We commonly find unsuspected entrapment of the posterior interosseous nerve secondary to a dorsal carpal ganglion. Although these cysts may be quite small and clinically occult, they routinely arise from the dorsal fibers of the scapholunate ligament near the junction of the lunate, scaphoid, and capitate bones and are well seen on MR imaging if the scan is examined carefully in this region. These cysts arise just deep to the radial aspect of the fourth extensor tendon compartment where the posterior interosseous nerve is receiving the sensory fibers of the wrist joint. MR imaging may demonstrate an associated small defect of the scapholunate ligament in patients with these dorsal ganglion cysts. The lower extremity: As in the upper extremity, MR imaging is commonly performed to exclude a mass along the course of a given nerve in patients with a specific neuropathy. MR imaging can also be used to evaluate lower extremity nerves after penetrating or blunt trauma. We have seen neuromas related to iatrogenic injury of the infrapatellar branch of the saphenous nerve from knee arthroscopy as well as neuromas related to iatrogenic injury of the deep peroneal nerve from ankle arthroscopy. We have also diagnosed a neuroma of the lateral calcaneal branch of the sural nerve related to repair of the Achilles tendon as well as a neuroma of the superficial peroneal nerve related to a fascial release for compartment syndrome. We have also seen nerve injury related to deep lacerations and fractures in the lower extremity with MR imaging. In the hip, it is relatively uncommon to encounter entrapment neuropathies with MR imaging. Enlargement of the iliopsoas bursa may be associated with hip arthropathies and can cause entrapment of the femoral nerve that can be detected with MR imaging. Rupture of the proximal hamstring tendons or muscles can result in a hematoma that entraps the sciatic nerve. The sciatic nerve may also be injured in posterior dislocation of the hip. We have seen several cases of obturator nerve entrapment by masses such as paralabral csyts resulting in adductor compartment muscle denervation on MR imaging. In the knee, we occasionally see entrapment of the peroneal nerve secondary to ganglion cysts that communicate with the proximal tibiofibular joint. These ganglion cysts may dissect along the course of small articular nerve branches to enter the nerve sheath and entrap the nerve fibers from within the sheath. Such intraneural ganglion cysts may occur near other joints but most commonly involve the common peroneal nerve. Intraneural ganglion cysts may rarely involve the tibial nerve and communicate with the knee. Amputation stump neuromas are well seen with MR imaging and are most commonly seen about the knee. In the ankle, we occasionally see entrapment of the tibial nerve in patients with tarsal tunnel syndrome. Common etiologies seen on MR imaging include talocalcaneal coalition that may undermine the tarsal tunnel and ganglion cysts that arise from the medial aspect of the ankle joint, subtalar joint or flexor tendon sheaths. Ganglion cysts and coalition may coexist and contribute to entrapment of the tibial nerve within the tarsal tunnel. Morton's neuromas are also well seen with MR imaging and are commonly found between the metatarsal heads in patients with forefoot pain. Most Morton's neuromas are decreased in signal on T2- weighted images, with increased signal seen in about 25% of cases on MR imaging. The increased signal may be related to intraneural edema and ischemia in relatively acute cases. Morton's neuromas may be detected with MR imaging in patients that are asymptomatic. This is not surprising given the existing body of literature that has determined the prevalence of various disorders such as spinal stenosis, disc protrusions, labral tears, and meniscal tears in elderly, relatively asymptomatic patients with MR imaging. Neoplasms of peripheral nerves: Any type of mass may compress a peripheral nerve and cause symptoms related to pain and dysfunction. In this section, we will briefly consider MR imaging of intrinsic masses of peripheral nerves.
