N2  - Globoid cell leukodystrophy (GLD), including the infantile phenotype Krabbe disease, results from a deficiency in the hydrolytic enzyme galactosylceramidase (GALC), which is responsible for degrading the central and peripheral nervous system (CNS, PNS) myelin lipids galactosylceramide and galactosylsphingosine (psychosine). Symptoms in infants include irritability and stiffness with disease progressing rapidly to a vegetative state and death in early childhood. Signs in terrier dogs include ataxia and spastic paralysis with disease progression warranting euthanasia at 16.2 +/- 4.5 weeks. Serum GALC concentrations in both children and dogs are decreased and psychosine concentrations are elevated. Conduction velocities in peripheral nerves of GLD dogs were < 50% of normal and G-ratios of the sciatic nerve were significantly higher than normal. Brain stem auditory evoked response testing exhibited a loss of waveform integrity and increased central conduction time consistent with auditory systemdemyelination. MRI of the brain showed T2-weighted hyperintensity of the white matter, widened sulci, and enlarged ventricles and MRS identified decreases in Nacetylaspartate and increases in choline, indicative of neuronal loss and abnormal myelin turnover respectively. Diffusion tensor imaging established decreased fractional anisotropy and increased radial diffusivity in multiplewhitematter tracts including the corpus callosum and posterior internal capsule. Histologically, GLD dogs revealed severe loss of myelin, a decrease in oligodendrocytes, astrogliosis, and microgliosis. Efficacy of hematopoietic stem cell transplant (HSCT) alone and in combination with intravenous adeno-associated virus (AAV) gene therapy is currently being evaluated in GLD dogs. Preliminary data suggests that addition of IV infusion of AAV substantially increases survival as compared to HSCT alone. Additionally, IV infusion of AAV in combination with CNS-directed delivery is being evaluated in GLD dogs in the absence of HSCT. Preliminary data demonstrates a clear dose response to the therapy, ability to prolong survival, and systemic delivery has shown a positive affect on treating PNS disease.
