N2  - Objective: Current medicines for diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN) control not the mechanism but the symptoms. Stem cell therapy has shown regenerative effects on damaged cells from limb ischemia, and some animal stem cells have been also reported to have favorable effects on DPN. Therefore, we explored the effects of human mobilized mononuclear cells (hMNC) in rats with DPN and examined the mechanisms. Method(s): hMNC were isolated and cryopreserved from a healthy volunteer after G-CSF mobilization. Sprague Dawley nude rats were induced to diabetes by streptozotocin injection and then 8 weeks later, 1 x 106 hMNC were transplanted into muscles of unilateral hind limb. Into the contralateral limb, normal saline was injected as a control. Before and at 4 weeks after transplantation, nerve conduction velocities (NCV) and laser-doppler perfusion imaging of both limbs were assessed. Immunohistochemical staining for PGP9.5 in the foot epidermis was performed to measure nerve regeneration. The grafts were monitored using 3T-MRI in vivo, and expression of growth factors with angiogenic and neurotrophic properties such as hepatocyte growth factor (HGF), nerve growth factor (NGF), and neurotrophin-3 (NT-3) were examined in the muscle by RT-PCR. Result(s): The STZ-induced diabetic rats showed decreased NCV and perfusion compared to nondiabetic rats. NCV, PGP9.5 density, and perfusion were improved in the hMNC-transplanted limbs compared to the saline-injected limbs in the diabetic rats. According to MRI imaging, iron-tagged MNC were observed as long as 5 weeks after transplantation, but the grafts didn't seem to exist so long. In the hMNC-transplanted muscle, mRNA expressions of the growth factors were increased compared to those in the salineinjected muscle. Conclusion(s): This study suggested that hMNC had a potential to regenerate the damaged nerves in DPN. Improved blood perfusion and induction of growth factors could be involved in the process.
