T1  - MR microneurography: A non-invasive tool for morphometric study of the tibial nerve
T3  - 38th European Society of Neuroradiology Diagnostic and Interventional Annual Meeting, ESNR 2015. Naples Italy.
A1  - Felisaz P.
A1  - Balducci F.
A1  - Gitto S.
A1  - Montagna S.
A1  - Maugeri G.
A1  - Vitale R.
A1  - Busi V.
A1  - Pichiecchio A.
A1  - Baldi M.
A1  - Calliada F.
A1  - Bastianello S.
Y1  - 2015//
N2  - Purpose: To characterize and quantify the tibial nerve gross anatomy with an acquisition and segmentation protocol of Magnetic Resonance (MR) microneurography using 3D Spoiled Gradient Echo (SPGR) with IDEAL sequences. Method(s): 14 healthy volunteers were investigated at the ankle level to obtain axial MR images of the posterior tibial nerve. Imaging was performed on a Discovery MR750 3T scanner (GE Healthcare, USA). Acquisition parameters: IDEAL 3D SPGR with TR 16 ms, TE 6 ms, flip angle 10degree, matrix size 512 x 420, FOV 5 cm, slice thickness 2 mm for a coverage of 2.8cm, bandwidth 25 kHz, NEX 1, time scan approx. 7 min. The nerve was manually segmented by two experienced operators (PFF, SB) in the IN Phase image, where the nerve boundary was clearly visible. This region of interest (ROI) was used to mask the nerve in the water image, where 2 class segmentation was performed using FSL FAST 1. Fascicular volume (FV), epineurial volume (EV), nerve volume (NV), and fascicular to nerve volume ratio (FNR) were quantified in the 2.8 cm covered using partial volume estimation. Inter-observer agreement was computed using interclass correlation coefficient (ICC). Result(s): MR microneurograms demonstrated the various components of the nerve such as the external and internal epineurium, the nerve fascicles and even the perineurium. In the 2.8 cm covered, NV average was 276 mm3. The mean FV was 192 mm3 (range 124-251 mm3). The mean EV was 83mm3 (range 52-137 mm3). The mean FNR was 0.69% (range 61- 76%). The interobserver reliability was excellent for both FNR and NV (ICC of 0.975 and 0.967 respectively). Conclusion(s): Morphological detail usually achieved with conventional MR neurography is insufficient for automatic segmentation of nerve structure. We propose an acquisition and segmentation protocol for reliable quantification of peripheral nerve structural parameters thanks to the high resolution ofMRmicroneurography. NVand FVmaymeasure nerve and fascicular enlargement reflecting edema. EV may be correlated with intraepineurial adipose proliferation in chronic neuropathies. Despite of a limited coverage, our results are promising and suggest a possible role of MR microneurography in characterizing the spatial distribution and temporal evolution of peripheral nerve damage.
