T1  - A case of brachial plexopathy in a patient with H1N1-associated acute respiratory distress syndrome: Prone positioning related injury or infiammatory complication?
T3  - 38th European Society of Neuroradiology Diagnostic and Interventional Annual Meeting, ESNR 2015. Naples Italy.
A1  - Gerevini S.
A1  - Caliendo G.
A1  - Colombo S.
A1  - Falini A.
Y1  - 2015//
N2  - Purpose:We describe theMRI features in a case of brachial plexopathy in a 39 year old patient, in intensive care unit (ICU) for ARDS (acute respiratory distress syndrome), related to H1N1 pneumonia. Method(s): Approximately three weeks after the onset of symptoms, the patient underwent an MRI scan on Philips Achieva 1.5 T with Head - Neck 16 channels coil. Sequences: TSE T2-weighted, T1w, DP fat saturated, STIR, THRIVE, DWIBS, T2 dixon, T1w dixon with and without contrast injection. Result(s): On MRI examination presence of diffuse STIR hyperintensity of the right brachial plexus, extended from the roots (C5-C8) to the terminal branches, with an increase in volume of the (C6, C7) roots and the upper and middle trunks. Scan demonstrated mild edema and minimal enhancement of the supraspinatus. On the left side slight hyperintensity on STIR of C5 root and the upper primary trunk. Conclusion(s): Our patient developed severe ARDS due to H1N1 pneumonia; he underwent prone positioning in the ICU. After discontinuation of sedatives he complained of right shoulder pain.Neurological examination confirmed a severe motor neuropathy. Right shoulder x-ray showed no bone fracture but a small widening of the acromioclavicular joint. His symptoms improved over the next 2 weeks. In literature only a few case reports of brachial plexus injury after prone position in the intensive care unit are reported. Peripheral nerve injury is thought to be the result of nerve ischaemia from undue stretching or direct pressure. Brachial plexus injuries most frequently affect the supraclavicular region rather than the retroclavicular or infraclavicular levels. The roots and the trunks are more commonly injured. However, in our case both supraclavicular and infraclavicular structures showed signal hyperintensity on STIR. Furthermore T2 hyperintensity was present bilaterally even if with asymmetric presentation. We think that an inflammatory pathogenesis could better explain the MRI features. Although we found some case reports of brachial plexopathy in patients affected by viral infections or bacterial sepsis, we didn't find any reports on patients with H1N1, but curiously several cases occurring after flue vaccination (trivalent).
