T1  - Brain maladaptive plasticity in small fiber neuropathy: Links between skin nerve degeneration and brain imaging signatures
T3  - 2015 Peripheral Nerve Society Biennial Meeting. Quebec City, QC Canada.
A1  - Chiang M.-C.
A1  - Hsieh S.-T.
Y1  - 2015//
N2  - Pain and sensory symptoms, such as dysesthesia or burning feet, of neuropathies have been traditionally attributed to alteration of the peripheral nervous system, i.e., peripheral sensitization. Several lines of evidence have indicated that parameters of peripheral nerve degeneration, for example intraepidermal nerve fiber (IENF) density on skin biopsies, could not completely explain the presence of pain in neuropathies. These observations raised the potential effects of peripheral nerve degeneration on the supraspinal structures. Previous research on neuropathic pain mainly focused on one level of neural axis, for example, at peripheral nerves or in the brain. It is not clear whether there existed brain plasticity alterations in peripheral neuropathies. To address this issue, this study examined the peripheral nerve terminals and the brain imaging on patients with small fiber neuropathy (SFN) by combining skin biopsy and functional MRI on 19 SFN patients compared with 17 healthy controls. We applied tensor-based morphometry to detect brain structural alterations in SFN. Greater volume reduction in pain-processing regions, particularly the bilateral anterior cingulate cortices (ACC), was associated with greater reduction of IENF density, serving as a pathological biomarker of skin nerve degeneration. We further applied psychophysiological interaction (PPI) analysis to assess functional connectivity of the ACCs during noxious heat stimulation. There was significant reduction in functional connectivity from the ACCs to the limbic structures (the parahippocampal gyrus and the posterior cingulate cortex), pain-processing area (the insula), and visuospatial areas (the cuneus). Furthermore, the degree of reduction in functional connectivity for the ACC to the amygdala and the precuneus was linearly correlated with the severity of IENF depletion. These observations suggest that SFN is not a pure PNS disorder. The pain-related brain networks tend to break into functionally independent components, with severity linked to the degree of skin nerve degeneration. These patterns provide imaging signatures of skin nerve degeneration-induced brainmaladaptive plasticity.
