N2  - Learning Objectives 1. To review capabilities of PET/MRI for response assessment, diagnosis, and prognostication of central and peripheral nervous system diseases. 2. To discuss new and evolving perspectives related to PET/MRI for imaging neurologic disorders. Clinical PET/MRI is now available and promises superior diagnostic performance compared to PET/CT for neurological applications. PET/MRI provides higher soft tissue contrast resolution and improved capability of disease detection and characterization, along with functional imaging capability and decreased radiation dose. In particular, PET/MRI may be useful to assess patients with behavioral neurodegenerative, vascular, traumatic, psychiatric, oncologic, ischemic, seizure-related, and age-related conditions of the nervous system, and for evaluation following use of cellular and gene therapies. In this presentation, we will discuss the following topics: 1. Advantages provided by PET/MRI relative to PET/CT for evaluation of neurological diseases: i. Improved soft contrast and spatial resolution ii. Decreased radiation dose iii. Functional imaging capabilities a. Diffusion-weighted imaging and diffusion tensor imaging b. Perfusion-weighted imaging c. Functional MRI d. Magnetic resonance spectroscopy 2. Clinical applications of PET/MRI in patients with neurological diseases i. Visualization and assessment of normal structures and substructures of the central nervous system: a. Hippocampus b. Thalamus c. Brainstem nuclei ii. Evaluation of central nervous system neoplasms a. Detection and localization b. Characterization c. Biopsy guidance d. Pretreatment planning e. Response assessment iii. Evaluation of neurodegenerative and psychiatric disorders iv. Detection and evaluation of seizure foci v. Evaluation of disorders of the peripheral nerves.
