T1  - Quantification of sciatic nerve pathology in inherited neuropathies via magnetization transfer mri
T3  - 66th American Academy of Neurology Annual Meeting, AAN 2014. Philadelphia, PA United States.
A1  - Dortch R.
A1  - Dethrage L.
A1  - Hamilton A.
A1  - Gore J.
A1  - Smith S.
A1  - Li J.
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - OBJECTIVE: To evaluate the potential of magnetization transfer (MT) MRI for assessing proximal nerve damage in inherited neuropathies.BACKGROUND: Recent clinical trials have emphasized the need for novel biomarkers capable of assessing treatment response and disease progression in inherited neuropathies, especially in proximal nerves that are inaccessible via current techniques (e.g., electrophysiology, skin biopsy). Magnetization transfer ratio (MTR) MRI has been shown to be sensitive to pathological changes in central nerves associated with myelin and/or axonal damage; however, no studies have evaluated MTR as a potential biomarker in peripheral nerves in vivo.DESIGN/METHODS: MRI was performed at 3.0 T (Philips Achieva) in twenty healthy volunteers (23-58 y.o.) and fifteen neuropathy patients from the following three cohorts: i) nine CMT1A patients (dysmyelinating CMT subtype, 28-56 y.o.); ii) two HNPP patients (dysmyelinating inherited neuropathy, 42-65 y.o.); and iii) four CMT2A patients (axonal CMT subtype, 26-57 y.o.). Fat-suppressed reference and MT-weighted data were collected from mid-thigh to the knee. The sciatic nerve (SN) was manually defined and slice-wise MTR values were calculated as MTR=(1-SMT/S0)*100%, where SMT and S0 are the MT-weighted and reference signal intensities. Clinical disability was assessed via clinical neuropathy scores. RESULT(S): From test-retest acquisitions (n=6), MTR values were found to be reproducible in the SN (p=0.70, paired t-test). The influence of BMI, age, and sex on MTR was insignificant in the healthy cohort (ANOVA, p>0.19). Mean MTR values were lower in CMT1A (35.2+/-3.0; p=0.07, t-test) and CMT2A (31.1+/-1.7%; p<0.01) patients compared to controls (37.1+/-2.3%). A significant relationship between MTR and neuropathy scores was also detected (p=0.03, Pearson's correlation coefficient=-0.62). CONCLUSION(S): MTR, which is sensitive to myelin and axon pathologies, may be a viable biomarker for proximal nerve pathology in inherited neuropathies. We are currently enrolling additional patients to assess whether multi-parametric MRI (quantitative diffusion and MT) improves sensitivity and specificity over MTR measurements alone.
