T1  - Advanced brain MR study in mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalomyopathy: Double dissociation between severe CIPO, mild neurological, and severe neuroradiological findings
T3  - 1st Federation Neurogastroenterology and Motility Scientific Meeting. Guangzhou China.
A1  - Tonon C.
A1  - Gramegna L.L.
A1  - Manners D.N.
A1  - Testa C.
A1  - Bianchini C.
A1  - Rinaldi R.
A1  - D'Angelo R.
A1  - Latorre R.
A1  - Stanghellini V.
A1  - De Giorgio R.
A1  - Lodi R.
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - Objective: Mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalomiopathy (MNGIE) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss of function mutations in the nuclear gene that encodes tymidine phosphorilase (TP). This leads to a marked reduction (or lack) of TP activity causing mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) defects and respiratory chain dysfunction. MNGIE is characterized by severe gut dysmotility, along with neurological impairment, i.e. ptosis, ophthalmoparesis, peripheral neuropathy, and brain leukoencephalopathy (i.e. diffuse white matter - WM - changes) without cognitive deterioration. This study is aimed to elucidate the pathophysiology of leukoencephalopathy in MNGIE patients using advanced MR techniques, such asMR spectroscopy (MRS), and diffusion tensor imaging (DTI). Method(s): Seven patients with a molecular diagnosis of MNGIE (3 F, 4 M; age range: 22-38 years) and 14 healthy controls (7 F and M, age range: 20-40 years) were studied using a 1.5 T GE scanner. Proton MRS was used to assess in brain WM concentrations of N-acetylaspartate (NAA), marker of neuronal and axonal integrity; creatine (Cr); and choline (Cho), component of membranes phospholipids. DTI was acquired to quantify several parameters of water molecules diffusion, such as mean diffusivity (MD). Group differences were calculated using the Student's t-test. Correlations were performed between 1H-MRS and DTI parameters, using the Pearson test one-tailed (p < 0.05). Result(s): In all patients a diffuse confluent cerebral leucoencephalopathy, often involving the sub-tentorial region, was detected. In patients WM [NAA], [Cr], and [Cho] were lower (p < 0.05) and WM MD higher (p < 0.05) vs controls. WM MD values were negatively correlated with WM [NAA] (p < 0.05). The reduction in all 1H -MRS metabolites in the brain WM of MINGIE patients can be explained by a dilution effectdue to increased brain water content demonstrated by the increased WM MD values. Conclusion(s): MNGIE patients showed a functional alteration of the blood brain barrier and absence of detectable brain axonal/neuronal loss, demyelination, and gliosis, thus supporting previous anatomopathological findings detected on some cases in a post-mortem analysis.The correlations between MRS and DTI changes suggest that MR parameters are robust biomarkers of disease, usable for monitoring the efficacy of therapeutic interventions.
