T1  - Measuring the unmeasurable: Mri quantification of disease progression in CMT1A over 12 months
T3  - 2013 Meeting of the Peripheral Nerve Society. Saint-Malo France.
A1  - Morrow J.M.
A1  - Sinclair C.D.J.
A1  - Fischmann A.
A1  - Hanna M.G.
A1  - Thornton J.S.
A1  - Yousry T.A.
A1  - Reilly M.M.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Current outcome measures in Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (CMT) show insufficient sensitivity to change to be useful in clinical trials. We investigated the responsiveness of quantitative MRI in CMT1A. We studied 20 patients with genetically confirmed CMT1A (9 females, 11 males, age 46+/-14 years, BMI 25.4+/-4.9 kg/m2) and 20 matched healthy volunteers (9 females, 11 males, age 43+/-14 years, BMI 25.1+/-4.6 kg/m2). Participants underwent quantitative MRI of the lower limbs at 3T repeated at a 12 month interval. The three-point Dixon fat-water separation method was used to generate fat fraction maps which were analysed at the mid-calf by defining regions of interest within six lower leg muscles bilaterally from which mean fat fraction was extracted for each participant. Mean lower leg fat fraction was increased at baseline in patients (15.8+/-25.5%) comparedwith volunteers (1.6+/-1.0%, p<0.001). Over 12months mean fat fraction significantly increased in patients by 1.3% (SEM=0.6%, p<0.05), whereas the volunteer fat fraction was unchanged (mean change 0.0%, SEM 0.1%, p=NS). The increase in fat fraction was similar across all muscles analysed: tibialis anterior +1.6%, peroneus longus +1.0%, lateral gastrocnemius +1.6%,medial gastrocnemius +1.4%, soleus +1.2%, tibialis posterior +1.1%. Subgroup analysis was performed in patients according to baseline fat fraction levels, classified as normal (<5%, n=8) or increased (>5%, n=9). Progression in one year was greater in those with increased baseline fat fraction (annual change +2.3%, SEM 1.1%) than those with normal baseline fat fraction (annual change +0.2%, SEM 0.1%, p<0.01 for comparison). Calculated standardised response mean (SRM) of mean lower leg fat fraction was 0.52 indicating moderate responsiveness over 12 months. This compares very favourably to currently used outcome measures such as the CMT Neuropathy Score which had SRM of 0.07 over 2 years in the placebo arm of the British-Italian Vitamin C trial. Responsiveness of MRI could be further increased by selecting moderately affected patients, or by quantifying fatty infiltration in distal calf or foot muscles for mildly affected patients. Increasing study follow up to two years will also increase responsiveness and this is underway. MRI provides the most responsive outcome measure identified to date for adults with CMT1A.
