T1  - Mr neurography: In-vivo detection of lower limb nerve injury in hereditary transthyretin familial amyloid polyneuropathy
T3  - 2013 Meeting of the Peripheral Nerve Society. Saint-Malo France.
A1  - Kollmer J.C.
A1  - Hund E.
A1  - Hornung B.
A1  - Schonland S.
A1  - Hegenbart U.
A1  - Kimmich C.
A1  - Kristen A.
A1  - Bendszus M.
A1  - Pham M.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Transthyretin familial amyloid polyneuropathy (TTR-FAP) is an autosomal-dominant transmitted life-threatening multisystem disease, leading to misfolding and extracellular deposition of the protein transthyretin. With an incidence of about 0.1-1/m, it is rare in Germany and the US, nevertheless it is the most common form of hereditary amyloidosis with endemic appearance, e.g., in Portugal and Sweden. An axonal, distal-symmetric sensory-motor, and autonomic polyneuropathy (PNP) is one of its main manifestations (Hund et al., Neurology, 2001; Plante-Bordeneuve and Said, Lancel Neurol, 2011). We investigated in-vivo amyloid related nerve injury by large-coverage and high-resolution MR-Neurography prospectively in a sample of 20 patients with genetically confirmed TTR-FAP (13 with manifest polyneuropathy (measurements included: complete neurological examination, scoring for NIS-LL (Neuropathy- Impairment-Score-Lower-Limbs), NDS (Neuropathy- Deficit-Score), NSS (Neuropathy-Symptom-Score), and detailed neurographic measurements), and 7 neurological/ electrophysiological asymptomatic gene-carriers) in comparison to 60 healthy, age/gender-matched volunteers. Imaging was done on a 3T-MR-scanner (Magnetom/Trio/Siemens) with high-resolution T2-weighted fat-suppressed-sequences separately for each leg and lumbar plexus: 1) axial T2- TSE-fs-sequences from proximal thigh to distal ankle (140 slices/leg; TE/TR 55/5970m); 2) 3D-T2-IR-SPC-sequence with axial reformation for lumbar plexus and spinal nerves (TE/TR 202/3000). Manual segmentation of the lumbar plexus/sciatic/tibial/fibular/sural nerve as nerve-tissue-voxels was performed on each axial imaging slice (320/subject). Lesion-voxels were then classified fully-automated, operator-independent and statistical analysis was performed by using nested-two-way-ANOVA and pairwise contrast analysis. On quantitative evaluation, lesion-voxel number was significantly higher not only in symptomatic TTR-FAP (16556+/-3297; p<0.0001) but also in asymptomatic gene carriers (5728+/-1794; p=0.02) compared to healthy controls. Differences in lesion-voxel number between symptomatic and asymptomatic TTR-FAP were also highly significant (p<0.0001). Furthermore, intraneural lesion-voxel count showed a predominant proximal focus (thigh) of nerve lesions (proximal 35290+/-3275 vs. distal 12610+/-1380; p<0.0001). For the first time, lower limb nerve lesions in hereditary TTR-FAP were detectable in-vivo by using high-resolution MRN not only in patients with manifest PNP, but also in asymptomatic gene-carriers in whom detection on MR-Neurography precedes clinical/electrophysiological manifestation. This suggests that MRN can serve as a novel and sensitive diagnostic marker even of early asymptomatic stages of TTR-FAP. Furthermore, we found a clear proximoto- distal gradient in nerve lesions in all 20 TTR-FAP subjects, although symptoms prevail distally and not proximally,maybe leading to possible pathomechanisms in TTR-related PNP.
