T1  - Diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) to assess proximal nerve integrity in patients with inflammatory neuropathies
T3  - 2013 Meeting of the Peripheral Nerve Society. Saint-Malo France.
A1  - Mathys C.
A1  - Aissa J.
A1  - Antoch G.
A1  - Meyer zu Horste G.
A1  - Turowski B.
A1  - Hartung H.-P.
A1  - Sheikh K.A.
A1  - Lehmann H.C.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - One of the major drawbacks in the management of patients with inflammatory neuropathies is the lack of objective and non-invasive measures to assess functional integrity of nerve fibers in proximal nerve segments. Such measures would be extremely useful to assess disease progression during treatment and to predict potential for recovery. Based on previous findings in pre-clinical studies (Lehmann et al., 2009), we investigated the utility of diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) MRI for detecting neuropathic changes in proximal nerve segments in patients with inflammatory neuropathies (CIDP and GBS). A total of twenty-one individuals with and without neuropathy (mostly CIDP) underwent DTI of a defined sciatic nerve segment. Patients and controls were evaluated by clinical examination and nerve conduction studies at baseline and six months after the initial DTI scan. Some patients received a second scan six months after the initialMRI.We found that themean fractional anisotropy (FA) value was significantly lower in sciatic nerves from patients with peripheral neuropathy as compared to controls. FA values correlated with clinical disability scores and electrophysiological parameters of axonal damage at baseline and 6 months after MRI scan. We conclude that DTI-derived FA values are a sensitive measure to discriminate healthy from functionally impaired human sciatic nerve segments. DTI of proximal nerve segments might be useful for estimating the proximal axonal degeneration burden in patients with inflammatory neuropathies, particularly in those who do not have distal sensory or motor evoked potentials. It might be therefore useful to identify patients who might be more eligible for neuroprotection trials to impede axonal degeneration.
