T1  - Diffusion tensor imaging and tractography of the sciatic nerves in chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy
T3  - 3rd Annual Meeting of the Associazione Italiana Sistema Nervoso Periferico. Verona Italy.
A1  - Filosto M.
A1  - Todeschini A.
A1  - Cotelli M.
A1  - Rinaldi F.
A1  - Rota S.
A1  - Padovani A.
A1  - Gasparotti R.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (CIDP) is an acquired peripheral neuropathy of autoimmune etiology. The diagnosis is based on the combination of clinical, electrodiagnostic and laboratory features. MR imaging showing bilateral hypertrophy of brachial and/or lumbar plexuses may support the diagnostic suspicion. Diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) has been recently proposed as a noninvasive technique to evaluate the peripheral nerve microstructure in vivo. We tested if diffusion tensor tractography (DTT) and measurement of fractional anisotropy (FA) along the sciatic nerves can differentiate patients with CIDP from controls. We therefore performed a 1.5 T MRI (Aera, Siemens, Germany) on 10 patients affected by CIDP (mean age 50.2) and 10 age- and sex-matched healthy volunteers (mean age 48.7) using the following imaging protocol: a) 3D magnetic resonance neurography (3D MRN) of brachial and lumbosacral plexuses, b) 2D MRN, and c) DTI of sciatic nerves at the level of thigh. DTT of the sciatic nerves was guided by color-coded FA maps overlaid on high resolution MRN. The FA values of the tracked segments of the sciatic nerves were compared between the two groups. The sciatic nerve cross sectional area (CSA) was also measured at the level of mid-thigh on the axial 2D MRN sections. The CSF protein concentration was available for 9 patients. T-test and Pearson correlation test were utilized for the statistical analysis. Threshold for significance was defined as p<0.05. In the patient group, plexus hypertrophy and increased signal intensity were detected in 13 out of 20 plexuses (65%) on 3D MRN. Sciatic nerve CSA was significantly increased in CIDP patients (mean 58.50+/-3.41 mm2) compared to controls (mean 86.20+/-22.07) (p=0.04). The sciatic nerve FA values were significantly lower in CIDP patients (mean 0.404, 95% C.I 0.378-0.427) than in healthy volunteers (mean 0.466, 95% C.I. 0.443-0.490) (p=0.001) and inversely correlated with the CSA values (r=-0.708, p=0.033). The association between the CSF protein concentration and FA values reached statistical significance (r=0.67, p=0.52). Our preliminary data suggest that noninvasive DTI assessment of the sciatic nerves may provide clinically useful information in addition to MRN in patients with CIDP. The quantitative assessment of FA can help further characterizing the effects of the disease processes on the structural integrity and directional coherence of the nerve fibers.
