A1  - Flaherty M.
A1  - Grant P.E.
A1  - Gupta M.L.
A1  - Engle E.C.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Background: Identifying and understanding primary errors of human axon guidance is complicated by the anatomical complexity of the central nervous system and the phenotypic complexity of disease processes. Congenital fibrosis of the extraocular muscles (CFEOM) is a rare, Mendelian complex eye movement syndrome, which can be caused by primary errors of axon guidance. Since eye movements are controlled by anatomically well-defined motor axons, and abnormal eye movements are easily discerned, CFEOM offers a unique opportunity to understand how human axon guidance goes awry. Here, we identify the genetic, molecular, and neurodevelopmental features of a novel CFEOM subtype, characterized by intellectual disability, polymicrogyria, and corpus callosum hypoplasia. Methods and Results: We used linkage analysis followed by DNA sequencing to identify an inherited heterozygous missense mutation in the beta-tubulin isotype TUBB2B that segregates with CFEOM, polymicrogyria, and intellectual disability. To determine whether this mutation causes primary axon guidance abnormalities or abnormalities secondary to diffuse brain malformation, we first performed diffusion tensor imaging (DTI). DTI reveals aberrations in commissural projection neuron trajectory, implying a paucity of homotopic connections. We next expressed exogenous Tubb2b- E421K in a mosaic fashion in developing mouse callosal projection neurons and found this to be sufficient to perturb homotopic connectivity, without affecting neuronal production or migration. Finally, by introducing the human mutation into yeast beta-tubulin, which is over 90% homologous with human beta-tubulin, we find that TUBB2B-E421K alters microtubule dynamics. Conclusion(s): These data provide evidence that TUBB2B mutations can cause primary axon guidance errors in the central and peripheral nervous system. Eight mutations in the closely related gene TUBB3 also disrupt microtubule dynamics and result in CFEOM. The shared molecular and neurodevelopmental features of TUBB3-CFEOM and TUBB2B-CFEOM support a convergent mechanism of axon dysinnervation caused by abnormal dynamic properties of neuronal microtubules.
