T1  - Peripheral nerve tractography using MR DTI
T3  - 2013 AANS Annual Meeting. New Orleans, LA United States.
A1  - Kliot M.
A1  - Narvid J.
A1  - Noss R.
A1  - Chin C.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Introduction: It has been a challenge to devise non-invasive imaging methods that can visualize axons in peripheral nerves. We have successfully applied MR DTI (Diffusion Tensor Imaging) to visualize nerve fibers in the clinical setting of traumatic nerve injuries and peripheral nerve tumors. Method(s): 14 patients underwent MR neurography that included two DTI sequences on a 3T whole body GE DVMR750 system including a full field of view single-shot EPI sequence with 28 diffusion weighted direction-encoded images and a reduced field of view diffusion method with 12 direction-encoded images that uses a 2D echo-planar RF pulse to excite and read out a rectangular-shaped field of view. Additional anatomic images were acquired as part of a routine MR neurogram protocol. Result(s): MR DTI successfully visualized nerve fibers within the capsule of peripheral nerve tumors. MR DTI also visualized nerve fibers and their absence in traumatized peripheral nerves. Intraoperative visual inspection and electrophysiological testing confirmed the location and relation of nerve fibers to tumors, as well as the presence and absence of nerve fibers in damaged peripheral nerve. Conclusion(s): MR DTI was used to visualize the relationship of axons to peripheral nerve tumors which can be helpful in the planning of surgical resections. It is also proving useful following traumatic nerve injuries to help determine the extent of axonal injury and may prove useful in determining whether axonal regeneration is occurring which might reduce the necessity for exploratory surgery.
