N2  - Introduction Intrinsic foot muscle atrophy is already present in a non-neuropathic stage of diabetes and increases with the severity of peripheral neuropathy [Greenman et al, 2005; Andersen et al, 2004]. The atrophy of small foot muscles may result in prominent metatarsal heads, toe deformity and limited joint mobility. These problems have been associated with increased plantar forefoot pressure, a major risk factor in plantar ulcer formation (Pham et al, 2000). The present study examines whether the toe flexor strength and the muscle volume of the intrinsic foot muscles in the diabetic neuropathic foot can be increased using a targeted training. Methods Fourteen diabetic patients (6 f/ 8 m; 56 +/- 7.2 years; Diabetes Typ I/II: 6/8; Diabetes duration 16.6 +/- 9.7 years) performed a toe flexor training with 70% of the maximal voluntary isometric contraction on a custom made dynamometer for eight weeks, three days per week, four sets per session, twelve repetitions (3s loading/ 3s relaxation). The maximal plantar flexion moment of the metatarsophalangeal joint (max MMPJ) was quantified on both feet before and after the intervention. The volume of the intrinsic foot muscles (VIFM) in the right forefoot was examined before and after the intervention using magnetic resonance imaging (1.0 T) with a circumferential extremity coil and, was analysed with manual segmentation on a section using software (ImageJ). The stage of peripheral neuropathy was assessed by the Neuropathy Disability Score (NDS) and Semmes-Weinstein monofilaments (SWMF). Results Seven patients were classified as neuropathic with moderate to severe peripheral neuropathy (NDS >= 6; SWMF >= 5.07/10g). The toeflexor strength increased significantly by about 55.3% on the left and 57.4% on the right foot following the intervention (fig. 1). Due to severe fat infiltration and substantial degeneration of the intrinsic foot muscles (fig. 2), the VIFM was only examined in seven subjects. The VIFM increased in these subjects by about 5.1% (pre: 51.3 cm3, post: 54.6 cm3). Discussion Before the intervention, the diabetic patients exhibited less than one third of the values for toe flexor strength of young healthy subjects [Goldmann et al 2011]. The increase in toe flexor strength was found in both neuropathic and nonneuropathic patients. One can speculate that enhancements in toe flexor strength and in intrinsic foot muscle volume may contribute to decreased peak plantar pressures, thus reducing the risk of ulceration in the diabetic neuropathic foot. (Figure Presented).
