T1  - Assessment of dysmyelination in charcot-marie-tooth disease type 1A via magnetization transfer ratio MRI
T3  - 64th American Academy of Neurology Annual Meeting. New Orleans, LA United States.
A1  - Dortch R.
A1  - Alexander R.
A1  - Li J.
A1  - Gore J.
A1  - Smith S.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Objective: i) To develop MTR protocols of the human sciatic nerve (SN) in vivo and ii) to apply these protocols in patients with Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A (CMT1A) to assay dysmyelination. Background Recent clinical trials in patients with CMT1A have raised the need for sensitive, noninvasive biomarkers to track disease progression. Magnetization transfer ratio (MTR) MRI has been shown to be sensitive to myelin content in the central nerves. However, no studies have used MTR to study peripheral neuropathies in humans in vivo. Design/Methods: MTR was measured in three healthy volunteers (28-36 y.o.) and three CMT1A patients (54-60 y.o.) in an axial volume immediately proximal to the knee. Volumes were acquired with/without an MT-prepulse (25 ms, 1000degree, 1.5 kHz off-resonance) at 0.8 x 0.8 x 3 mm3 resolution, yielding 45 slices in 6 min. Fat was suppressed via a binomial pulse. Regions of interested (ROIs) were selected for the SN and mean slice-wise MTR values were calculated: MTR = 1- SMT/S0, where SMT/S0 are signal intensities with/without the MT-prepulse. Result(s): High-resolution MTR imaging of the SN was achieved at an adequate signal-to-noise ratio (aD;40 for non-MT-weighted images) and level of fat suppression. The mean (+/-SD) MTR over all slices was reduced in CMT1A patients (0.33+/-0.01) relative to controls (0.37+/-0.02). A length-dependent reduction in MTR was also observed in one of the CMT1A patients (aD;25% lower values in distal relative to proximal portions of the SN). The slice-wise MTR values were relatively constant in the other two CMT1A patients and in the controls. Conclusion(s): MTR imaging is feasible and may detect pathological changes in the proximal nerves of CMT1A patients. We are enrolling a larger cohort of patients with CMT1A in which MTR values and diffusion metrics (sensitive to axonal degeneration) will be correlated with electrophysiological findings and disability scores.
