A1  - Dalla Torre C.
A1  - Campagnolo M.
A1  - Cacciavillani M.
A1  - Manara R.
A1  - Briani C.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Hypertrophy of spinal nerve root and peripheral nerves trunks is suggestive of a demyelinating polyneuropathy and is present in more than half of patients affected with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (CIDP). We report a 75-year-old who had been complaining of slowly progressing distal paresthesias at four limbs and gait impairment for the last 5 years. Neurological evaluation showed bilateral foot drop, distal hyposthenia at lower limbs (tibial anterior MRC 3/5 on the right side and 2/5 on the left side, extensor hallucis longus MRC 3/5 on the right side and 0/5 on the left side, gastrocnemiousMRC4/ 5 bilaterally), lower limbs and distal upper limbs hypotrophy and abolished deep tendon reflexes at lower limbs. Neurophysiological evaluation revealed a chronic sensory-motor demyelinating polyneuropathy associated with secondary axonal degeneration. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showed elevated protein level (502 mg%). CIDP was diagnosed. A peripheral nerve ultrasound evaluation was then performed revealing diffuse and significant enlargement of nerve cross sectional area (CSA) at four limbs, both proximally and distally: right ulnar nerve CSA 75 mm2 at wrist (normal values <=6 mm2), 91 mm2 at forearm (normal values <=8 mm2), 149 mm2 at arm (normal values <=11 mm2), right peroneal nerve CSA 48 mm2 at fibular head (normal values <=14 mm2) and 81 mm2 at peroneal fossa (normal values <=16 mm2), left sural nerve CSA 9 mm2 at median third of the leg (normal values <=3 mm2). Spinal MRI showed also diffuse thickening of the spinal roots, especially at the cervical level (diameter of 13 mm), and also of the lumbar plexus (root diameter 12 mm). After gadolinium injection a mild root enhancement was present. In our patient the findings were exemplary of nerve trunk and root hypertrophy in CIDP and both ultrasound and MRI confirmed the diagnosis.
