T1  - Potential and pitfalls of diffusion tensor imaging in emergency radiology
T3  - 2012 Annual Scientific Meeting and Postgraduate Course of the American Society of Emergency Radiology, ASER 2012. New Orleans, LA United States.
A1  - Niogi S.
A1  - Comunale J.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Purpose/Aim: To provide an overview of the potential role of DTI in acute trauma, how to interpret DTI findings, and recognize its limitations. Content Organization: DTI has been used in acute conditions such as spinal cord injury, traumatic brain injury (TBI), and nerve transection. A clear understanding of the potential role of DTI in emergency radiology and its limitations is necessary before DTI can be used clinically. Potential uses: Spinal cord ischemia and trauma: In cases of acute spinal cord injury, clinical CT and MRI often underestimate the extent of injury. Spinal cord DTI tractography, however, demonstrates disruption or complete interruption of normal fiber anatomy in these cases. Potentially, DTI tractography can be used to facilitate a more specific diagnosis for acute spinal cord injury. Nerve transection: DTI can be used to individually delineate cranial nerves and peripheral nerves such as the median nerve. DTI can be used to facilitate diagnosis of complete versus partial nerve transection. TBI: Conventional MRI underestimates the degree of white matter damage. DTI can potentially better elucidate post traumatic axonal injury. In the acute setting, DTI can be used to better diagnose severity of damage and potentially help distinguish between accidental and nonaccidental pediatric trauma. Unfortunately, numerous pitfalls exist currently limiting its use clinically. These include: influence of hardware and sequence parameters on DTI metrics necessitating a normal database for comparison, no agreement as to best analysis techniques, inability to assess chronicity of findings, and inability to directly attribute many DTI findings to a particular etiology. Summary/Conclusion: DTI is more sensitive to neuronal changes than current clinical MR techniques and has a potential role in emergency radiology. It is important for radiologists and trainees to begin understanding the utility and limitations of DTI since it is a powerful technique that is starting to enter the clinical scene.
