N2  - Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (CIDP) is a relapsing or chronic progressive peripheral neuropathy. Thickened peripheral nerves or enlarged nerve roots have been detected in subpopulations of CIDP, exhibiting unique symptoms such as compression myelopathy. However, it is not well understood why nerve hypertrophy occurs only in some patients with CIDP, and not in others. To clarify the characteristics of hypertrophic form of CIDP, we assessed the clinicopathological features of 8 patients with CIDP who had extensive peripheral nerve hypertrophy who were referred to our institution. All patients fulfilled the criteria for CIDP proposed by Ad Hoc Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology AIDS Task Force (1991), and were negative for genetic abnormality for Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A. Patients consisted of six men and two women, aged 33 to 70 years. The duration from onset of neuropathy to diagnosis widely ranged from 2 to 21 years (mean 12.9 years). Diffuse or focal hypertrophy was found in the nerve roots in six patients and peripheral nerve trunk in six patients. Abnormal enhancement by gadolinium at the site of hypertrophy was confirmed by magnetic resonance imaging in six patients. Patients tended to show sensory-dominant neuropathy pattern with sensory ataxia. Cranial nerve involvement was reported in three patients. Cerebrospinal fluid protein was markedly elevated (mean 411 mg/dL) in most patients without an increase of cell count. Nerve conduction studies revealed marked slowing of motor nerve conduction velocities and prolongation of distal latencies in all patients. Temporal dispersion also was evident. Sensory nerve action potentials were not evoked in six patients. Sural nerve biopsy specimens revealed onion bulb formation in seven of eight patients. Slow insidious progression of clinical symptoms, particularly predominant in sensory symptoms, tended to lead to delayed initiation of treatment. In conclusion, a prolonged course without treatment could have induced extensive nerve damage of demyelination and subsequent hypertrophic change. Alternatively, these patients might intrinsically tend to develop nerve hypertrophy based on some unknown genetic susceptibility, resulting in a clinical and pathological variant of CIDP of a certain pathologic background, different from that in typical CIDP.
