T1  - Primary progressive dysarthria without aphasia as a variant of frontotemporal lobe degeneration
T3  - 22nd Meeting of the European Neurological Society. Prague Czechia.
A1  - Grznarova M.
A1  - Hofstadt-van Oy U.
A1  - Wolf S.
A1  - Strotzer M.
A1  - Oschmann P.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Frontotemporal lobe degeneration (FTLD) can lead to dementia with early progressive personality changes and behavioural problems or distinct clinical subgroups like primary progressive aphasia or semantic dementia. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) may demonstrate lobar degeneration; post mortem neuropathology is heterogeneous with detection of tau-positive and tau-negative patients. Case report: A 71 year old retired female teacher in otherwise good health presented with a two year history of progressive dysarthria without any swallowing problems. Personality, cognitive function and intellectual interests were maintained as activities of daily living as witnessed by patient and her husband. Unarticulated verbal expression had led to some social retreat and moderate depression. Neurologic examination on admission revealed a severe dysarthrophonia with impaired articulation movements and was otherwise normal, a laryngoscopy showed normal pharyngeal and vocal chord anatomy. Neurophysiologic examination of peripheral nerves and muscles including the tongue were normal as were magnetic stimulation including triple stimulation technique to hand- and leg muscles. No decrement was found with repetitive stimulation of accessory and facial nerves, no response to articulate abilities was seen with injection of a cholinesterase inhibitor. Serum antibodies (Acetylcholine receptor, Muscle specific kinase, Voltage gated calcium channel) were negative; the spinal fluid was normal including tau protein. MRI revealed a frontotemporal atrophy especially of the left perisylvian region, a cerebral scintigraphy showed a left temporoparietal hypoperfusion. On formal speech testing no deficits in perception or naming were detected, minimal deficits were seen in generation of words. Reading perception and writing were intact. On neuropsychologic testing moderate deficits in verbal and visual memory and mild deficits of attention and executive functions were detected. Conclusion(s): Though MRI and brain perfusion studies were compatible with primary progressive aphasia as a variant of FTLD, our patient did not have aphasia on speech testing while neuropsychologic testing was compatible with a mild dementia still compensated in daily living. If the university education and the maintained intellectual interests of our patient might have masked aphasia or if the presented case represents a distinct subgroup of FTLD may be subject of future analysis of cases with FTLD.
