T1  - Miller fisher syndrome occurring 14 years after guillainbarre syndrome
T3  - 15th Congress of the EFNS. Budapest Hungary.
A1  - Szoke K.
A1  - Aranyi Z.
A1  - Takats A.
A1  - Bereczki D.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Background: The spectrum of acute antibody mediated conditions affecting the peripheral nervous system includes a number of conditions, such as Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) and Miller Fisher syndrome (MFS). There is evidence that the mechanism of GBS, particularly the acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN) variant and of MFS involves the production of antibodies to gangliosides and ganglioside complexes, induced by Campylobacter jejuni infection, and considered as a true case of molecular mimicry. It is therefore not surprising that the symptoms of GBS and MFS may overlap. Here, we present a case where the two conditions occurred sequentially. Patient(s): A 39-year-old male patient, following an episode of diarrhoea, presented with severe bilateral ophthalmoparesis, ataxia, areflexia, and dysarthria, highly suggestive of MFS. The diagnosis was supported by auxiliary examinations: MRI of the brain was normal, cerebrospinal fluid examination showed mildly elevated protein content (0.75g/L), and nerve conduction studies revealed low amplitude sensory potentials. Final confirmation was given by the detection of anti-GQ1b antibodies in the serum. The patient underwent a course of plasma exchange therapy, and after an initial worsening (appearance of facial palsy) recovered within 6 weeks. Fourteen years earlier, this patient was diagnosed with GBS, when he experienced an acute onset of weakness in his legs. He recovered completely. Conclusion(s): This case demonstrates that GBS may recur in the same patient, although in its MFS variant. It is speculated that susceptibility to GBS or its variants is increased in certain individuals, perhaps related to their particular ganglioside makeup.
