T1  - DTI to assess degeneration and regeneration in traumatic nerve injuries
T3  - 2011 Meeting of the Peripheral Nerve Society. Potomac, MD United States.
A1  - Sheikh K.A.
A1  - Zhou Y.
A1  - Sen M.
A1  - Paker A.M.
A1  - Narayana P.A.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Serious injuries to the peripheral nerves often result from civilian and combat trauma. The patients with traumatic nerve injuries are left with significant disability and morbidity. There is an acute need for reliable measures to monitor nerve injury and repair to make informed management decisions in clinic. Currently, noninvasive techniques that allow early assessment of regeneration in traumatic nerve injuries are not available in clinical practice. We are developing magnetic resonance (MR) based diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) technology, to monitor the degenerative and regenerative response in patients with traumatic nerve injuries. We hypothesize that alterations in DTI parameters can detect nerve fiber degeneration and regeneration. To test this hypothesis, we have studied seven normal controls and four patients with complete (Sunderland grade V) traumatic nerve injuries of median and ulnar nerves in the forearm. All the patients with traumatic neuropathies had surgical repair of injured nerves, underwent clinical, electrophysiologic, and MRI/DTI studies. MRI sequences included T1- and T2-weighted (SPAIR with fat sat), STIR, and DWI. The fractional anisotropy (FA) maps were generated and the longitudinal and radial diffusivities were quantified. We found that FA maps delineate the forearm nerves much more clearly than other sequences. FA values for uninjured median and ulnar nerves were 0.75 +/- 0.03 and 0.71 +/- 0.04, respectively, and did not change significantly along the length of the nerve. Imaging of the injured nerves showed that the highest FA values were seen in the proximal stump of the repaired nerves. The denervated distal stumps of the injured nerves had dramatic changes in the DTI derived measures. Nerve segments adjacent to repair site undergoing reinnervation had increased FA compared to the completely denervated caudal segments of distal stump. These findings show the potential role of DTI as a quantitative and useful technology in assessing degeneration and regeneration in traumatic nerve injuries.
