N2  - As potential treatments become available for Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease 1A (CMT1A), accurate ways to measure disease progression are needed to test treatment efficacy. The 'three-point Dixon' fat/water separation method is a quantitative magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) technique to measure the proportion of fat within a given tissue. We performed lower limb MRI including three-point Dixon sequences of the mid calf of 14 patients with genetically confirmed CMT1Aand 16 healthy volunteers. We also performed isometric myometry (CSMi HUMAC Norm Testing System) of ankle plantarflexion, dorsiflexion, inversion and eversion. Mean muscle fat percentage was increased in CMT1A patients compared with controls in all muscles assessed: tibialis anterior 18.4% vs. 1.3%, peroneus longus 21.2% vs. 2.8%, lateral gastrocnemius 14.4% vs. 1.8%, medial gastrocnemius 16.9% vs. 2.3%, soleus 14.0% vs. 2.6%, tibialis posterior 14.6% vs. 1.5%. Five CMT1A patients had fat percentages within the normal range for all muscles, whereas 2 had greater than 50% fat in all muscles. Of the 7 patients with abnormalities of some muscles only, peroneus longus and tibialis anterior were most commonly involved. There was good correlation between quantitative fat measurements and qualitative assessments of T1-weighted images. Correlation between muscle fat and ankle strength was calculated in CMT1A patients using Spearman rank correlation: tibialis anterior and ankle dorsiflexion: rho = -0.55, p < 0.001, ankle plantarflexors (soleus, medial and lateral gastrocnemius) and ankle plantarflexion: rho = -0.64, p< 0.001, peroneus longus and ankle eversion: rho = -0.641, p = 0.001, tibialis posterior and ankle inversion rho = -0.084, p = 0.70. In conclusion, these data demonstrate that calf muscle fat in patients with CMT1A is increased compared with healthy volunteers with tibialis anterior and peroneus longus preferentially involved. Moreover, there is a strong correlation between muscle weakness and muscle fat. Quantitative muscle fat measurement using MRI may be useful as an outcome measure in CMT1A.
