A1  - Heinen C.
A1  - Pedro M.
A1  - Antoniadis G.
A1  - Wirtz C.-R.
A1  - Konig R.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Introduction: Diffusion tensor tractography (DTT) of peripheral nerves is a new technique to evaluate tissue changes after injury. This study demonstrates the feasibility and clinical application of this technique in the preoperative assessment of traumatic brachial plexus injuries (BPI). Method(s): Preoperative diffusion tensor imaging was performed in a total of 80 brachial plexus in 10 healthy volunteers and 30 patients with BPI. 60 DTI slices were acquired in the axial plane (slice thickness 2. 7mm, b values = 0 and 900 s/mm2, and 64 gradient directions). FA, ADC maps and tensor calculation were performed in-line by the MRI scanner. DTI fiber tracks were calculated and analyzed. All patients underwent physical examination and electrophysiological testing. Intraoperative findings were correlated with diagnostic results to evaluate the accuracy. Result(s): In all volunteers the roots C5, C6, C7 brachial plexus were clearly identified on DTT, however in C8 and Th1 the nerve course could be only inconsistently reconstructed. In 23 patients DTT showed a significant asymmetry between the injured and the healthy contralateral brachial plexus. Surgical exploration confirmed the preoperative MRI findings in all patients. Supraclavicular BPI could be correctly excluded in 4 patients. Conclusion(s): DTT is technical feasible for the evaluation of postganglionic lesions of BPI with high accuracy at the level of C5, C6, and C7. It reveals an insight into microstructural organisation of peripheral nerves and allows distinction of unaffected nerve tissue and neuroma. Thus, DTT has promising potential in the evaluation and monitoring of BPI.
