Y1  - 2011//
N2  - MRI imaging is a very useful tool in peripheral nerve disorders investigation. Different MRI image regimens can be used for structural nerve study. With special fiber tracking software, it is possible to visualize neural tracts with diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) data. There are few studies in which peripheral nerves have been visualized with DTI. Our objective is to characterize nerve trunk abnormalities according to tractography in different nerve diseases. All patients were submitted to conventional MRI in a 3 Tesla scanner (Siemens Magnetom Trio Tim, Erlangen, Germany) including T1 (TR:590, TE:21), T2 SPACE 3D (TR:1500, TE:162), STIR (TR:5340, TE:68, TI:200) and fat-saturated proton density with pulse restore (TR:1980, TE:39) weighted images. To provide morphological study of the nerve and diffusion-weighted, echo planar imaging (EPI, b = 900) pulse sequence was done in the axial plane, through 8.5 cm of the nerve and the values of the fractional anisotropy and mean apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) were obtained. Case 1: A 38y.o. male had a multifocal inflammatory neuropathy. Diffusion-weighted images in the elbow showed thickening of the ulnar nerves with hyperintense signal in proton density images. Case 2: A 42 y.o. female presented bilateral ulnar neuropathy due to leprosy. Tractography in the bilateral ulnar nerve had normal aspect on conventional images. Case 3: A 35 y.o. female with severe wrist pain after surgery for carpal tunnel syndrome; the wrist and the median nerve was studied and presented normal on conventional images. Case 4: A 52y.o. male with demyelinating multifocal features performed images throughout the entire sciatic nerves. There was moderate thickening in the proximal segment, with hyperintense signal in STIR images. Axial proton density fat-saturated images of the left leg showed hyperintense signal in the hallucis flexor longus muscle, indicative of denervation. Case 5: A 45 y.o. man with traumatic ulnar neuropathy. CB was depicted in this nerve at the elbow. Tractography revealed an enlarged ulnar nerve due to edema. Tractography can be used to image and visualize peripheral nerves. It could provide useful information for diagnosis and follow-up of nerve lesions, including entrapments, inflammatory processes, hypertrophic inherited disorders, edema and regeneration.
