A1  - Morisaki S.
A1  - Oda R.
A1  - Fujiwara H.
A1  - Kawata M.
A1  - Kubo T.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - To evaluate the sensitivity of diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) in assessing peripheral nerve regeneration in vivo. We assessed the changes in the DTI parameters and histological analyses after nerve injury to examine degeneration and regeneration in the rat sciatic nerves. Serial MRI of the right leg was performed at the time points of 1, 2, 3 and 4 weeks after the crush injury. The changes in fractional anisotropy (FA), axial diffusivity (AD) and radial diffusivity (RD) were quantified. For histological analyses, the number of axons and the myelinated axon areas were quantified. Increased FA and decreased RD were observed in the regenerative phase. The changes in FA and RD were strongly correlated with histological changes, including axonal and myelin regeneration. DTI parameters, especially RD, can be good indicators for peripheral nerve regeneration and can be applied as non-invasive diagnostic tools for a variety of neurological diseases.
