T1  - Semi-automatic plexiform neurofibroma tissue segmentation
T3  - Computer Assisted Radiology and Surgery - 25th International Congress and Exhibition, CARS 2011. Berlin Germany.
A1  - Weizman L.
A1  - Hoch L.
A1  - Ben Sira L.
A1  - Joskowicz L.
A1  - Pratt L.-T.
A1  - Constantini S.
A1  - Ben Bashat D.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Purpose: Neurofibromatosis-1 (NF1) is a common genetic disorder associated with the frequent occurrence of central and peripheral nervous system tumors [1]. Plexiform neurofibromas (PN) are one of the hallmarks of NF1. These are peripheral nerve sheath tumors which consist of (Figure presented) proliferation of cells in the nerve sheath, extending along the length of the nerve and involving multiple nerve fascicles [2]. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is the method of choice for noninvasive assessment of tumors, as it plays an essential role in classification and grading, preoperative evaluation, follow-up, and therapeutic management. The scan protocol of MRI consists of several conventional and advanced pulse sequences. STIR (Short-T1 Inversion Recovery) is the common pulse sequence to detect and characterize PN. In this paper, we present a new method for segmentation of PN from STIR MRI scan. The method utilizes a user-based initial delineation of the tumor area in a single slice to automatically detect the PN lesions in the entire image. Our method requires less user initialization for the segmentation process, most of the segmentation is performed automatically. Method(s): The method consists of five steps: * PN Region of interest selection-the user is required to coarsely delineate the lesions area in a single slice. * Slices intensity normalization-The higher intensity values of every slice in the image are normalized with the higher intensity values of a selected slice. * Automatic tumor detection in a single slice-The PN pixels in each slice are automatically detected with histogram-based analysis on the selected ROI using a previously published method [3]. * 3D region growing-Based on the unique nature of this specific tumor, the segmentation results of the previous step propagates to the other slices in the image automatically. * Manual adjustment-the user manually eliminates or adds certain areas in the image to the final PNs segmentation. Figure 1 illustrates the steps of our method. Result(s): The method was tested on seven datasets with PN lesions. We performed two studies: in the first study, we examined the total segmentation time and accuracy of our method. This study yields a mean volume overlap difference of 25% as compared to manual segmentation by expert radiologist. The average computation time of our method including the manual user interaction time was 12 min. In contrast, the mean manual annotation time exceeds one hour. In the second study, we performed an analysis of the resulted overlap error versus the user interaction time invested by the user. As a result we obtained that approximately 5 min of user interaction are required to achieve a volume overlap error less than 25%. Figure 2 shows the segmentation results of our method versus manual segmentation results. Conclusion(s): We have presented a new method for the semi-automatic segmentation of PN lesions in STIR MR images. The current manual segmentation of PNs is time consuming, error prone and may require several hours for a single dataset. Our method is unique in that it requires less user interaction than manual or other semi-automatic PN (Figure presented) segmentation methods. The proposed segmentation method is much more user friendly. An analysis of the performance of the method versus the manual user interaction time leads to the conclusion that only several minutes of user interaction are required to obtain a reliable PN segmentation. In the future, we plan to extend the method to handle also whole-body STIR scans and to add the FLAIR (Fluid-Attenuated Inversion Recovery) pulse sequence to eliminate the CSF and other cystic regions. This process will improve our results and reduce the false-positive errors of our method.
