T1  - Intrathecal baclofen (lioresal) infusion to the patient with persistent vegetative state after traumatic brain injury: Two cases with video presentation
T3  - 10th World Congress of the International Neuromodulation Society: Drug Delivery and Peripheral Nerve. London United Kingdom.
A1  - Jung H.H.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Introduction: There were sporadic case reports about recovery from persistent vegetative state after infusion of baclofen intrathecally. We present our experience of two patients who were improved their consciousness only after intrathecal infusion of baclofen with trial dose. Material(s) and Method(s): Baclofen (Lioresal) was infused intrathecally to the two patients who were persistent vegetative state for more than 6 months after traumatic brain injury. Only trial dosage, 50, 75, and 100 microgram were used, and we measured JFK Coma Recovery Scale-Revised (CRSR), SSEP, EMG, functional MR, perfusion CT with video record before and after infusion. Result(s): Case 1: This 33-year-old female patient was transferred from other hospital after traumatic brain injury. Her mentality was deep stuporous, with occasional nonpurposeful movement and visual fi xation, which was compatible with vegetative state. Her vegetative state was lasted 6 months after trauma, so we recommended intrathecal baclofen infusion trial for controlling spasticity. Her spasticity was responded to Lioresal and her recovery of consciousness was prominent, CRS-R improved from 4 to 11 immediate after trial period. And this phenomenon was lasted till in our hospital but without more improvement. Discussion(s): There were some eff orts to recover the patients with unconsciousness in the fi eld of functional neurosurgery, such as using deep brain stimulation or spinal cord stimulation. Intrathecal baclofen infusion also showed improvement in unconscious patients in some cases. There are some hypotheses of baclofen eff ect; modulating neuronal centripetal outputs, sleep-wake cycle modulation, and improving axonal conduction. But in our cases, we could not demonstrate these hypotheses. And in trauma patient, the extent of injury is not uniformed which will be problematic to choose this kind of trial. Conclusion(s): There are only few reports, and the protocol of baclofen infusion is not verifi ed. Also the mechanism of baclofen on recovery from vegetative state is not explained. Systemic trials are needed to establish the eff ectiveness of intrathecal baclofen in recovery of consciousness.
