A1  - Eligar V.S.
A1  - Rice S.
A1  - Williams T.D.M.
A1  - Rao S.
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - 23 years male T1DM for 18 years, necrobiosis lipoidica, peripheral neuropathy and multiple admissions with diabetes ketoacidosis presenting complaints H/O leg pains and weakness of the right leg prompting to use of a walking stick. An MRI of the right hip and examination under anaesthesia was conducted which did not reveal any abnormality. Then he presented with low GCS following overdose with cocodamol and alcohol, managed in ITU. He developed urinary retention and loss of temperature, pain and vibration sense along with motor weakness below T6 spinal level. An MRI scan showed the spinal cord changes consistent with acute myelopathy. With the history of sensory neuropathy and gradually progressive motor weakness in the right leg, myelopathy is secondary to diabetes. No other pathological process was identified. Microangiopathy of vasanervorum and effects of advanced glycation end products may explain the pathogenesis in this case. A group in Israel has reported 12 cases of spinal cord involvement secondary to diabetes by retrospective analysis of the data. Recently a diabetes research unit from Sheffield demonstrated significant difference in the spinal cross-sectional area between patient with diabetes and without DSP (diabetic sensory peripheral neuropathy) and in healthy subjects. There was smaller cord area in patient with diabetes with DSP compared to controls. Summary: Myelopathy in diabetes is not common but is the most likely in our case, and has previously been reported. Though the evidence surrounding this is not sufficient, we propose more data has to be created. We propose that in cases of atypical neuropathic symptoms imaging of the spinal cord may demonstrate abnormality.
