T1  - Application of large deformation diffeomorphic metric mapping to adrenomyeloneuropathy
T3  - 39th Annual Meeting of the Child Neurology Society. Providence, RI United States.
A1  - Risen S.R.
A1  - Gilford A.
A1  - Binstock A.
A1  - Wang J.
A1  - Zackowski
A1  - Fatemi S.A.
A1  - Naidu S.
A1  - Raymond G.V.
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - Objective: Adrenomyeloneuropathy (AMN) is an X-linked Adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) with onset in adulthood that presents with peripheral neuropathy and myelopathy. Despite primary involvement of specific spinal cord white matter tracts, recent evidence using Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) supports cerebral pathology in the corticospinal tract (CST) in patients with AMN. A newer image analysis tool, large deformation diffeomorphic metric mapping (LDDMM), allows for more rapid analysis of whole brain white matter. To enhance further exploration of cerebral pathology in AMN, we validated LDDMM results against DTIStudio analysis of the CST in a cohort of patients with AMN. Method(s): 5 mild and 5 severely affected AMN patients were studied; clinical severity was measured by the expanded disability status scale. Patients were scanned on Philips 1.5T MRI system, acquiring whole-brain DTI images. CST white matter integrity was analyzed using both DTIStudio and LDDMM. Result(s): In patients with AMN, the DTI-derived metrics (fractional anisotropy (FA), mean diffusivity (MD) and perpendicular diffusivity (lambda)) have been shown to differ significantly from control values (p<0.01) in the CST from the pons to thalamus, thus indicating a change in white matter integrity. The values for this section of CST are: FA = 0.54+/-0.05, MD = 0.88+/-0.02, l = 0.58+/-0.04, which will be compared to the LDDMM results. Conclusion(s): Application of the LDDMM method to brain imaging in patients with AMN will further enhance the understanding of cerebral pathology by allowing for rapid, complete analysis of brain white matter tracts.
