T1  - High-resolution ultrasound in a severe peripheral neuropathy with intraneural perineurioma: A special case
T3  - 54. Jahrestagung der Deutschen Gesellschaft fur Klinische Neurophysiologie und Funktionelle Bildgebung. Halle, Saale Germany.
A1  - Borchert A.
A1  - Paulus W.
A1  - Young P.
A1  - Schilling M.
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - Introduction: Progress in ultrasound techniques enables its use for diagnostics of peripheral nerve diseases. Sonography is useful for neuro-imaging to get additional information about peripheral nerves, especially if electrophysiology approaches are limited. In a special case of a severe neuropathy ultrasound provided important data and allowed short-term re-examination. Case-report: We present a 27-year-old female patient with a progressive sensorimotor neuropathy of the legs since her early childhood. Physical examination revealed a severe peroneal atrophy with high-grade paresis of extension and flexion of feet and a hypesthesia of the lower legs. Family anamnesis, genetic examination of CMT disease and laboratory results were normal. In the electrophysiologic testings nerve conduction velocities of the legs were not detectable. Neurography and sonography of the arms were normal. High-resolution ultrasound of the tibial nerves on both sides showed a hypertrophy with cross sectional areas about twice as large compared with the normal population. Based on these results, a selective high-resoulution MRI of the affected region was possible. It also showed a hypertrophy of the tibital nerves with contrast agent enhancement on both sides. Sural nerve biopsy showed an intraneural perineurioma on the right side and a severe axonal damage without indicating an inflammatory etiology on the left side. Discussion(s): High-resolution ultrasound of peripheral nerves enriches the diagnostic methods in peripheral nerve diseases. In the present case there was no response after electric stimulation of all nerves in the lower limbs which makes them unsuitable for further diagnostic information and re-examination. High-resolution sonography represents an alternative method which is rapidly available. The etiology of the polyneuropathy described above is presently unknown. It might be a rare manifestation of a hereditary neuropathy, whereas normal result of neurography and ultrasound of the upper limbs are uncommon. Usually the nerves of the arms are affected although they were clinically normal. The detection of an intraneural perineurioma in the right sural nerve might be an accidental coincidence, but the combination with homogenous cross sectional area enlargement of both tibial nerves might imply a multifocal and segmental intraneural peroneurioma as a reason of the polyneuropathy. A bilateral occurrence of this benign nerve tumor was described by Beekmann et al. (2004) located at the ulnar nerve. High-resolution sonography will be the method of choice to monitor the course of this peripheral nerve disease.
