T1  - 3T magnetic resonance imaging with DTI and HMRS in adult-onset krabbe disease: A case report
T3  - 25th Congresso Nazionale Associazione Italiana di Neuroradiologia, AINR. Firenze Italy.
A1  - Prudente M.
A1  - Tessitore A.
A1  - Cirillo M.
A1  - Giordano A.
A1  - Sorbo A.
A1  - Tortora F.
A1  - Cirillo S.
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - Aim of the study: Krabbe disease is an autosomal recessive leukodystrophy caused by a deficiency in the lysosomal enzyme galactocerebrosidase (GALC) responsible of the regular relationship between myelin breakdown and production, causing a severe demyelination of both the central and peripheral nervous system. The most frequent type of Krabbe disease has an infantile onset. Unusual slowly progressive adult forms have also been described. We described early involvement of the upper corticospinal tract in a case of a patient affected by late-onset form of disease. Material(s) and Method(s): A 25-year-old man was hospitalized with a 12 months history of progressive lower limbs wasting and weakness, gait unsteadiness with difficulty walking on uneven surfaces. Neurological examination showed mild weakness and wasting of all muscles of the upper and lower limbs with bilateral postural tremor. Tendon reflexes were normal at upper limbs and symmetrically increased at lower limbs with absent Babinski sign. The patient underwent magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain with high field scanner (3T). Proton magnetic resonance spectroscopy was performed at the level of the centrum semiovale with a point-resolved spectroscopy (PRESS) technique with long and short TE. Metabolites were analyzed in the areas of MR signal abnormalities and normalappearing brain. Thirtytwo-direction DTI and quantitative tractography of the corticospinal tracts were performed. Result(s): MRI revealed symmetrical selective increased signal intensity both on T2-weighted and FLAIR images, along the corticospinal tracts, extending from cortical-subcortical junction to the cerebral peduncles. The abnormal signal intensity was detectable also across the corpus callosum at the isthmus, which was thinner. MRS snowed a very mild elevation of Choline (Cho) and an abnormal peak at 3.6 ppm. N-acetylaspartate (NAA) and creatine peaks were normal both in gray and white matter. DTI with quantitative tractography detected significant loss of diffusion anisotropy in the corticospinal tracts. On biochemical investigaton homocysteine level was 56.7 micromoles liter (normal level < 13 umol/i). A low b-galatocerebrosidase level was detected in the serum and confirmed with lysosomal analysis of fibroblasts from a skin biopsy that showed a galactosylceramide b galactocerebrosidase level of 0.00 U/g (normal range 0.03-0.12). Conclusion(s): These results confirm the vulnerability of corticospinal tracts is the earliest neuroradiological marker of Krabbe disease in adult patients. Diffusion tensor-derived anisotropy maps provide a quantitative measure of abnormal white matter in patients with Krabbe disease and are more sensitive than T2-weighted images for detecting white matter abnormality.
