T1  - Chronic demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) and relapsing remitting central demyelination: Spreading autoimmunity from peripheral to central myelin?
T3  - 19th World Congress of Neurology. Bangkok Thailand.
A1  - Ariatti A.
A1  - Sola P.
A1  - Girolami F.
A1  - Canali E.
A1  - Galassi G.
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - Objective: CIDP is acquired neuropathy with progressions and relapses. Multiple sclerosis (MS) evolve with relapsing remitting, progressive course. Cases: Case 1: A female aged 18 developed facial weakness, limb paresthesias, imbalance. On 1st admission, brain MRI was normal. Ten years later she experienced dysarthria, tremor, acral paresthesias. On examination there were ataxia, briskreflexes, loss of perception. MRI revealed multiple spinal cord lesions. Electrophysiology showed demyelination. CSF was normal. Antibody assays were negative. Patient received high steroids i.v. IVIG was given periodically. Case 2: was diagnosed having CIDP when aged 34 and MS at 36. When aged 35 she developed renal failure due to nephritis. Her complaints were acral paresthesias, extremity weakness, recurrent imbalance, blurred vision. Patient exhibited tremor, ataxia, impaired sensation, areflexia. MRI showed numerous periventricular, brain stem lesions. CSF showed oligoclonal bands. CIDP of case 3: aged 48 presented with distal weakness, stepping gait, areflexia. Electrophysiology suggested demyelination, axonal loss confirmed by nerve biopsy showing epineurial T cell infiltrates. CSF had no bands. Type II diabetes was discovered one year later. Repeated IVIG gave benefit. Brain MRI showed multiple relapsing white matter lesions. Discussion(s): Few reported patients exhibit central and peripheral demyelination. Demyelination among CNS in MS spare peripheral nerves. CIDP with clinical CNS signs are rare. In our patients, neuropathy predated diagnosis of MS. There are similarities between immunepathogenesis of CIDP and MS which may result of interaction between genetic susceptibility, cellular and humoral responses directed against triggering antigens. In both an autoimmune process is initiated against same antigenic targets or by mechanism of cross reactivity and mimicry between antigens. Coincidental occurrence of multiple autoimmune disorders support concept of predisposing autoimmunity.
