T1  - Diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) to assess axonal regeneration in peripheral nerves
T3  - 2009 Meeting of the Peripheral Nerve Society. Wurzburg Germany.
A1  - Lehmann H.C.
A1  - Ji Z.
A1  - Mori S.
A1  - Sheikh K.A.
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - Development of outcome measures to assess ongoing nerve regeneration in the living animal that can be translated to man can provide extremely useful tools for monitoring the effects of therapeutic interventions to speed nerve regeneration. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a noninvasive technique that can be applied serially on the same subject to monitor pathophysiologic changes in different tissues. Diffusion tensor imaging (DTI), an MR imaging technique, is based on the movements of water molecules within biological tissues. In neural tissues water molecules tend to move along the nerve fibers and tracts. This motion of water molecules in a preferential orientation is called 'anisotropic diffusion' and DTI can create a new image contrast that shows locations of tissues with high anisotropy, namely nerve tracts. In this proof of concept studies, we examined the use of ex vivo high-resolution DTI for imaging intact and regenerating peripheral nerves in mice and validated the MRI findings with histological studies. DTI was done on sciatic nerves with and without crush at two different time points and in four different mouse strains. DTI measures, including fractional anisotropy (FA), parallel diffusivity, and perpendicular diffusivity were acquired and compared in proximal and distal segments of uninjured and crushed nerves and correlated with morphometry. A comparison of axon regeneration after sciatic nerve crush showed a comparable pattern of regeneration in the four different mice strains. In crushed sciatic nerves the average parallel diffusivity was significantly lower compared to uninjured sciatic nerve segments, indicating a decreased parallel motion of water molecules along the axons. Validation studies indicate that the FA values showed highest correlation with the total number of regenerating axons and the perpendicular diffusivity was negatively correlated with axon counts. These studies indicate that DTI is a sensitive measure of axon regeneration in mouse models and establish the foundations for further development of imaging technology for application to living animals and humans.
