T1  - Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) of the peripheral nerves
T3  - 134th American Neurological Association, ANA Meeting. Baltimore, MD United States.
A1  - George A.T.
A1  - Zhou Y.
A1  - Narayana P.A.
A1  - Sheikh K.A.
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - DTI is a magnetic resonance based technology that is particularly suited for imaging nerve fiber tracts. Because of limitations of current clinical and investigative measures to assess peripheral nerve injury and repair in some clinical situations, the need for non-invasive imaging of peripheral nerves that can reliably assess extent of nerve degeneration and regeneration is increasingly realized.Availability of such a technology can be immediately applicable to patients with traumatic nerve injuries to assess nerve injury and repair and in the area of therapeutics aiming to enhance nerve regeneration/ repair. With this in mind, we have developed acquisition and image analysis protocols to obtain quantitative measures such as fractional anisotropy (FA), parallel and perpendicular diffusivities, and FA-based 3D-reconstructions of the individual nerves. Eight normal controls underwent scanning of their lower legs or forearms with the goal to develop norms for different DTI parameters for further application to patients to differentiate the healthy and diseased nerves and monitor nerve regeneration/repair. Our results show the viability of this approach to image various nerves of different sizes in both upper and lower extremities. These ongoing studies support that this imaging modality has the potential to assess nerve injury and repair.
