T1  - Imaging of the cortico-spinal tracts in diabetic neuropathy using diffusion tensor magnetic resonance
T3  - 69th Annual Meeting of the American Diabetes Association. New Orleans, LA United States.
A1  - Wilkinson I.D.
A1  - Slevarajah D.
A1  - Hutton M.
A1  - Griffiths P.D.
A1  - Gandhi R.
A1  - Tesfaye S.
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - Results: The pathophysiology associated with diabetic neuropathy (DN) appears to be complex and is not fully understood. As well as peripheral nerve abnormalities, recent imaging studies have indicated spinal cord and brain involvement. The form and extent of changes to the spinalcorticosomatosensory pathways may well be important when determining disease mechanisms. Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) is a magnetic resonance (MR) technique that can measure the anisotropy of parenchymal water diffusion, reflecting cellular architecture, in-vivo. Information from DTI can yield models which reflect the make-up of axonal pathways (tractography) within the CNS. This preliminary study sought to provide an initial assessment of spinal-corticosomatosensory pathway involvement in DN monitored by MR diffusion tractography. High field (3T) diffusion MR Imaging has been performed on 6 non-diabetic healthy volunteers and 4 subjects with painless DN (Dyck's staging criteria). High angular-resolution DTI (32 directions) datasets have been acquired (1.75x1.75x2mm2 resolution) covering the entire cerebrum and cerebellum. Following data acquisition, diffusion tensor matrices were calculated from which tractography was performed [MedINRIA, www-sop.inria.fr]. Two regions of interest defined on the underlying anatomical images comprised: (i) right somatosensory cortex and (ii) brainstem. The mean number (+/-sd) of calculated tracts passing through both of these regions were: 193+/-1 in healthy volunteers and 170+/-28 in the DN group. As expected, all identified tracts passed through the thalamus. Minor cortical and cerebellar contralateral connectivity was apparent. These data indicate a trend suggesting abnormalities in intracranial parenchymal water diffusivity, consistent with representing a lower number of axonal fibre tracts. If proven significant, this implies that the involvement of the CNS is not limited to a length dependent axonal degeneration of first order but also involves second order neurons. Additional data is required to determine the significance of these preliminary findings. This information further underlines the need to map out CNS involvement in diabetic neuropathy.
